! Custody per face, the wave-two handbacks

Verbatim wave-two handbacks of four Luna-6 YIN and YANG rounds (2026-10-19); only Markdown transliterated to memetic-wikitext. Each round's two handbacks trade findings, so a later round confirms or refutes an earlier one, and round four outranks the rest. The rulings and the per-vessel table rest at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/custody-per-face-2026`>>.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r1-yin-opening`>>

YIN-Luna · merged F6 seal-fork handback · platform lens · accessed 2026-10-19

Evidence marks: MEASURED means read directly in cited source or on a cited vendor/spec page. LEAD means inference, an unverified detail, or a proposed design consequence. Claim provenance in the ratchet: confirmed-by-both, yours-alone, or twin-alone (TWIN.md = YANG code lens; MINE.md = YIN platform lens). Local paths below are repository-relative to `/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine`, unless a path begins `bags/`.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r1-yin-platform-ground`>>

!!! Platform ground


''MEASURED — lifecycle does not promise an awake tab or a continuously executing SharedWorker.'' HTML specifies SharedWorker sharing and ports, not a guaranteed execution schedule when clients become backgrounded/frozen. Chrome’s Page Lifecycle guidance documents frozen pages as suspending freezable task queues and its Energy Saver behavior can freeze eligible background tabs; Android Chrome explicitly warns SharedWorker instances can terminate unexpectedly. A discarded page has unloaded, so its JS cannot answer. Worker survival while all tabs freeze is implementation-dependent; there is no cross-engine guarantee that it will run, be throttled in a particular way, or remain alive. Sources, consulted 2026-10-19: [[HTML Standard, Shared workers|https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/workers.html#shared-workers-and-the-sharedworker-interface]]; [[Chrome Page Lifecycle API|https://developer.chrome.com/blog/page-lifecycle-api]]; [[Chrome freezing on Energy Saver, 2025-01-20|https://developer.chrome.com/blog/freezing-on-energy-saver]]; [[Chrome 148 release notes, stable 2026-05-05|https://developer.chrome.com/release-notes/148]]. The last source says Android SharedWorkers were long disabled over unpredictable process lifetimes and are being re-enabled while reliability is investigated. ''LEAD:'' treat a worker as volatile and make outstanding writes retryable or name their wait/refusal; do not make security depend on it waking.

''MEASURED — availability as of 2026-10-19.'' SharedWorker works in current desktop Chrome, Firefox, and Safari, and Safari on iOS supports it (MDN compatibility table/Baseline 2026). Chrome stable 148 re-enabled SharedWorker on Android on 2026-05-05, explicitly while lifecycle reliability remains under investigation. Firefox Android support appears in the MDN compatibility table; version availability can drift, so query runtime support rather than hard-code a cross-platform lifecycle assumption. Sources: [[MDN SharedWorker, accessed 2026-10-19|https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/SharedWorker]]; [[Chrome 148 release notes, 2026-05-05|https://developer.chrome.com/release-notes/148]]. The `SharedWorker` feature row does not guarantee equivalent process lifetime across desktop/mobile.

''MEASURED — no peer-death `MessagePort` close event contract.'' `MessagePort.close()` is a method that a script invokes to close its own endpoint; MDN documents it as disconnecting that port. MessagePort’s standard event model supplies message/messageerror, not a dependable remote-close event when a tab or process vanishes. Sources, accessed 2026-10-19: [[MDN MessagePort.close(), last modified 2025-09-18|https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/MessagePort/close]]; [[HTML Standard, MessagePort|https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/web-messaging.html#message-ports]]. ''LEAD:'' explicit disconnect improves normal teardown only; frozen, crashed, discarded, or killed tabs need not run it. Don’t infer tab death or wakefulness from a missing response except through an explicit named timeout policy—which this house forbids as a lock decision.

''MEASURED — Web Locks and BroadcastChannel do not identify an awake sealing tab.'' Web Locks coordinate/serialize same-origin scripts (including workers), and are useful for exclusive store ownership; a held lock does not promise its owner can promptly service a message. BroadcastChannel sends messages among eligible same-origin/storage-partition contexts; it has no authoritative roster, and silence cannot distinguish asleep, frozen, dead, or absent. Sources, accessed 2026-10-19: [[MDN Web Locks API|https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Web_Locks_API]]; [[MDN Broadcast Channel API|https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Broadcast_Channel_API]]; [[HTML Standard, Web Locks|https://w3c.github.io/web-locks/]]; [[HTML Standard, BroadcastChannel|https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/web-messaging.html#broadcasting-to-other-browsing-contexts]].

''MEASURED — same-origin scripts can attach to the SharedWorker.'' MDN says same-origin contexts can access a SharedWorker, and specifically warns that once created, any same-origin script can obtain a reference and communicate with it. The constructor URL must be same-origin with the caller’s document. Sources, accessed 2026-10-19: [[MDN SharedWorker|https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/SharedWorker]]; [[MDN SharedWorker constructor security considerations|https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/SharedWorker/SharedWorker]]; [[HTML Standard|https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/workers.html#shared-workers-and-the-sharedworker-interface]]. ''LEAD:'' origin alone cannot authenticate a trusted tab against same-origin injected/compromised script; the holder must bind each port to an admitted session and enforce allowed verbs. This is a threat-model inference, not a finding of a present vulnerability.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r1-yin-ratchet-claims-in-twin-md-checked-against-source`>>

!!! Ratchet: claims in TWIN.md checked against source


# ''confirmed-by-both, with a boundary. MEASURED:'' Browser `HOLDER_ROUTES` maps `custody:write` to `owner` (`packages/lararium-browser/src/holder-routes.ts:3-6,30`); the island route delivers owner messages only to current owner (`shared-holder-core.ts:117-125`). Browser vessel boot persists Keyhive/Veil archives through `saveBrowserKeyhiveArchive` / `saveBrowserVeilArchive` (`open-browser-vessel.ts:678-679,732-737`). `writeKeystoreRowHeld` obtains the unlocked VK for the `idbName` and calls `writeSealedCarrier` (`browser-custody.ts:386-402`). ''LEAD / not confirmed from these excerpts:'' exact `custody:write` shape `{carrier,bytes}`, whether it always means a full Keyhive export, and global absence of any browser handler; the owner route alone does not establish payload schema or exhaustive absence.
# ''confirmed-by-both. MEASURED:'' `writeSealedCarrier` accepts an absent row or one whose bytes open under the supplied VK (`sealed-writer.ts:213-218,279-288`). It seals replacement plaintext, writes and reads back a sidecar, opens that sidecar for validation, then renames (`:290-298`). Thus the writer opens/proves the previous same carrier on overwrite; it does not imply caller-level read-modify-write. The value supplied by caller may still be a whole replacement archive. TWIN’s correction is sound.
# ''confirmed-by-both, narrowly stated. MEASURED:'' browser custody rows in this path use `unlocked.get(idbName)` as VK (`browser-custody.ts:386-402`); sealed-store key table distinguishes vessel key and per-bag content keys (`bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store.mem:65-72`). Content key holder separately holds bare content keys only while unlocked and has a lock operation (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/content-key-holder.ts:2-17`). ''LEAD:'' do not generalize that every kind of private row in every store seals under this VK without a complete writer census. For the named browser custody carrier path, it is vessel-wide, not face-keyed.
# ''twin-alone, not established. LEAD:'' the specific node veil-row selection via vessel-wide `mountedFace` remains unverified in the inspected requested source. Browser archive helpers key Veil rows by handle (`browser-keyhive-archive.ts:86-94`, as cited in TWIN.md); that does not prove the node selector or refute it.
# ''confirmed-by-both, qualified. MEASURED:'' route owner-only (`holder-routes.ts:30`, `shared-holder-core.ts:117-125`). ''LEAD:'' “no browser handler exists anywhere” only follows after exhaustive registration/dispatch search; no such exhaustive result was established here.
# ''twin-alone / partly confirmed. MEASURED:'' `withCustodyRows` offers one exclusive browser read/decision/write act (`browser-custody.ts:404-417`); node behavior wires content-key operations into daemon behavior (`operator-daemon-behavior.ts:1034-1068`, as inspected in TWIN.md). ''LEAD:'' approximately 20 node `updateCustodyRow` callers and the full listed caller inventory were not checked in this pass. Keep them as separate vessel-thread verbs unless routed/atomicity is demonstrated; do not treat the island route as their universal write door.

''MEASURED — owner/session mechanics.'' Shared holder core stores a port set, session-to-port bindings, and one owner (`shared-holder-core.ts:92-105`). Owner routing drops with `no-owner` or delivers to that port (`:117-125`). `disconnect` removes a port/session and clears owner only when explicitly called (`:192-200`). `connect` assigns first live port as owner, boots the island, and binds a session envelope to the port (`:202-221`). Incoming messages are validated against owner epoch, session ID, bound port, and generation (`:223-250`). This is explicit core cleanup; no evidence says a `MessagePort` peer-death event invokes it automatically.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r1-yin-consequence-of-the-one-vk`>>

!!! Consequence of the one VK


''MEASURED:'' The operator’s rule gives each tab its own unlock; holder retains face content keys only while a same-face session remains unlocked, with last-session close locking that face (`bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/session-unlock-2026.mem:25-40`). The sealed-store table separates VK from per-bag content keys (`bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store.mem:65-72`). Browser custody obtains one VK by `idbName`, then uses it for the carrier (`browser-custody.ts:386-402`).

''LEAD — security interpretation:'' if the same VK opens every face’s custody carrier, (a) and (a′) constrain *which unlocked tab may invoke sealing* and keep the VK out of the SharedWorker, but they do not create cryptographic face isolation. Any authorized path holding that VK may open any row sealed under it. A per-face content key does not automatically solve custody archives: Keyhive archive/root material may be vessel-wide, whereas Veil archive rows can be face-specific. The ruling should ask for per-face custody wrapping/sealing keys for face-private rows, while explicitly naming vessel-wide rows and recovery semantics. Distinct face keys change what a compromised or mistaken session can open; a per-face route policy alone only narrows ordinary requests.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r1-yin-ranked-merged-verdict`>>

!!! Ranked merged verdict


# ''(a′) Asker seals — preferred for session-caused work, with row scope and queued wait.'' The cause tab already owns its unlock, so route a tab-caused sealed write back to that admitted, unlocked session. For sync/delegation with no asker, target an admitted unlocked session of the row’s face. If none responds/stands, leave a named `seal-awaits-session`; do not use a clock to lock or pretend Web Locks/BroadcastChannel prove wakefulness. This best matches one unlock per tab and no holder unlock. Current code’s owner-only route must change, and occupied-carrier proof means the chosen sealer still needs open authority under the current writer. ''Holds across:'' normal awake tab writes, concurrent tabs when each session remains admitted, quiet sync waiting, owner tab closing while another suitable session remains. ''Does not alone hold:'' face confidentiality under a shared VK; tab death without explicit disconnect; persistent write completion when all tabs freeze/close.
# ''(a) Owner tab seals — viable compatibility fallback.'' Current browser routing already sends the owner these requests. It keeps VK tab-side and has a simple custodian. It couples unrelated write availability to the manifest owner; frozen/discarded/dead owner may strand work and no dependable peer-close event repairs this. ''Holds across:'' owner awake, owner-unlock write flow. ''Flips upward:'' product rule gives owner exclusive authority and accepts waiting until owner reconnects. ''Flips downward:'' owner must not monopolize face-specific write rights or owner can close while other face sessions remain.
# ''(b) Seal-only facet — conditional future design.'' It could limit a capability to encrypt/sign and lower plaintext/VK exposure if the crypto and store protocol genuinely do not require opening the standing row. Current `writeSealedCarrier` explicitly opens the old carrier and sidecar under VK (`sealed-writer.ts:279-298`), so a pure non-opening sealer cannot perform this exact replacement proof. SharedWorker lifetime and same-origin port admission also remain concerns. ''Holds across:'' only after append-only/new-generation writes or an independent transition proof removes old-row opening, capability scope is face/operation limited, explicit lock revokes it, and port messages are session-authenticated. ''Flips upward:'' a verified non-opening, revocable, scoped primitive and storage protocol.
# ''(c) Tab-side sealed store — lowest under current one-Repo/one-owner law.'' It may create a stronger boundary if it also has distinct keys and face-specific custody stores, but merely adding a second store under the same VK does not. It adds locks, reconciliation and crash/consistency paths contrary to the single-store owner shape. ''Holds across:'' only if face-separated stores/keys become a required architecture and the house defines one authoritative writer/restore protocol for each.

''Biggest uncertainty / single settling probe:'' instrument one browser vessel with two faces A and B and two independently unlocked tabs. Write distinct archive carriers, then issue (i) A-caused replacement after the owner tab closes, (ii) B-caused replacement, and (iii) uncaused sync replacement while both tabs are frozen/discarded/closed in turn. Record which admitted session receives each request, whether old carrier opens under each proposed key, whether no-tab work waits with `seal-awaits-session`, and whether last-session close leaves no VK or face content key in the holder. Repeat a cross-face open attempt using distinct proposed per-face custody keys. This one probe settles routing/liveness and whether the current one-VK premise violates the required face boundary; it should use real target browser versions because docs do not promise worker scheduling.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r1-yang-opening`>>

YANG handback — F6 seal fork (code lens)

Evidence labels: MEASURED means directly present in source; LEAD means inferred or unverified. Repository paths are relative to /home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine. Read on 2026-10-19; no suites, builds, or git writes were run.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r1-yang-sharp-answer`>>

!!! Sharp answer


Yes: a VK-sealed custody carrier overwrite must open the previous same carrier when it exists. `writeSealedCarrier` permits landing only if the carrier is absent or the supplied VK opens it (packages/lararium-mesh/src/sealed-writer.ts:208-218); the write path opens the prior value, then writes a sidecar, reads/opens it back, and renames it (same file:17-23,279-309). This is a validation/open, not caller-side read-modify-write: callers supply a full replacement value. Browser `writeKeystoreRowHeld` serializes the full value, reads row state, obtains the unlocked VK, then calls this writer (packages/lararium-browser/src/browser-custody.ts:386-402). Composite custody callers can explicitly read and write under one act (`browser-custody.ts:404-417`), but a whole archive value does not merge with the old archive.

No: the daemon mesh's sealed content records do not update an old sealed row. They append/new-address lockboxes, signed records, ciphertext values, and index rows. `#mint` writes a fresh lockbox and key-version record (packages/lararium-mesh/src/content-key-holder.ts:383-421); blob seal writes ciphertext, manifest, index, and new head pointer (`:480-515`); receive lands supplied parcels/records before reload/index (`:717-736`); memory import writes fresh memory-import records and lockboxes (`:841-857`). It does read state to choose lineage, prior manifest-head parents, validate records, and index manifests, but does not open a previous row to rewrite that row. The manifest-head read at `:501-505` only decides the new manifest's parent list.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r1-yang-call-site-inventory-cause-read-key`>>

!!! Call-site inventory: cause, read, key


| Write | Cause | Prior read? | Required key |
| Vessel Keyhive Archive export | Boot/identity setup, not sync arrival (`packages/lararium-keyhive/src/operator-daemon-behavior.ts:1002-1033`; browser setup `packages/lararium-browser/src/open-browser-vessel.ts:678-679,732-737`) | Whole archive exported; no old archive RMW in persistArchiveFloor (`packages/lararium-keyhive/src/archive-floor-write.ts:120-148`). Sealed overwrite still opens same carrier. | VK seals row; Keyhive archive is the exported state. Locked archive bars export/write. |
| Veil Archive export | Boot/identity setup after veil stands (`operator-daemon-behavior.ts:1028-1033,1045-1049`) | Whole archive; same old-carrier proof on overwrite. | VK seals row; veil identity state is payload. Browser archives are indexed by handle (`browser-keyhive-archive.ts:86-94`). |
| custody:write | Kernel request; currently holder router sends it only to owner (`packages/lararium-browser/src/holder-routes.ts:23-31`), not asker | Payload-level RMW is absent for a whole carrier; destination writer opens current carrier for validation. | VK. Exact `{carrier,bytes}` protocol shape and provenance are LEAD until protocol definition is checked. No handler was found in browser sources; route alone does not prove no adapter elsewhere. |
| sealed-seal body + manifest | Explicit daemon verb/session act (`operator-daemon-behavior.ts:758-772`) | Reads staged body and content-key lineage; no prior body-row rewrite. New ciphertext and manifest. | Per-bag/version content key seals body/private record; session signer signs record. |
| New key-version + lockbox | Roll triggered by session action, seat roll, admission/removal, or holder operation (`content-key-holder.ts:383-421`; seat composed `operator-daemon-behavior.ts:1034-1064`) | Reads lineage/parents/readers; does not replace prior lockbox/record. | Content key signs record; Keyhive member seat wraps lockbox. VK persists the archive needed to reopen identity state. |
| Checkpoint, hand-over, admission records | Explicit daemon verbs; e.g. hand-over `operator-daemon-behavior.ts:807-817` | Reads plans/lineage, writes new record/lockbox. | Relevant content key + session signer; Keyhive seat for lockbox. |
| sealed-receive | Explicit local receive verb for remotely delivered member-lane data (`operator-daemon-behavior.ts:834-841`) | Lands ciphertext/records then reloads; no prior row open (`content-key-holder.ts:717-736`). | No key to copy ciphertext; content key to verify/open/index. Keyhive seat to open lockbox. |
| Outbox parcels | Explicit hand-over/parcels/serve verbs (`operator-daemon-behavior.ts:792-848`) | Fresh parcel seal, no old parcel open. | Separate outbox key; VK unlocks its wrapped key; content key/Keyhive operations provide parcel content. |
| Memory section cut | Explicit session request (`operator-daemon-behavior.ts:867-878`) | Reads currently held material to construct new section, no old sealed row update. | Bundle key seals section; payload carries content keys. |
| Memory import | Explicit session import (`operator-daemon-behavior.ts:887-902`) | Reads/verifies section and lineage; adds fresh signed memory-import records and lockboxes (`content-key-holder.ts:841-857`). | Imported content key signs; Keyhive seat wraps local lockbox; VK preserves archive. |
| Browser custody row write | Setup/custody act | Opens existing carrier as above (`browser-custody.ts:386-402`). | One VK for this idbName/store, including vessel and face-indexed archive rows. |

LEAD: a sync-triggered `sealed-rolls` catch-up may itself create roll writes (`operator-daemon-behavior.ts:907-909`); its writes should be verified as additions, which the content holder's write patterns suggest. A remote arrival itself is not currently shown autonomously invoking receive; the daemon verb is explicit.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r1-yang-lead-claim-review`>>

!!! Lead claim review


# Archive writes are whole-value exports: confirmed (`archive-floor-write.ts:120-148`; browser saves `browser-keyhive-archive.ts:82-94`). `custody:write` route-to-owner confirmed (`holder-routes.ts:23-31`). The exact payload cited from node source remains LEAD in this scope. Correction: storage layer itself opens an occupied carrier before overwrite, even though no read-modify-write rides the door.
# Carrier overwrite opens prior same carrier: confirmed (`sealed-writer.ts:11-23,208-218,279-309`).
# VK is per vessel, not per face: confirmed by key table (`bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store.mem:65-69`) and browser `idbName` VK lookup (`browser-custody.ts:386-402`). Thus face isolation for these rows is policy/session routing, not cryptographic separation. A session holding this VK can technically open any carrier it seals, including another face's archive.
# Node veil-row choice via vessel-wide mountedFace: not established by requested files. Browser rows use handleIndex (`browser-keyhive-archive.ts:86-94`); node identity-specific writer selection is provider-based (`operator-daemon-behavior.ts:1045-1049`). Treat mountedFace detail as LEAD.
# Browser route to owner is measured; no handler found in searched browser source, but absent-handler claim remains qualified by search scope.
# Node's many updateCustodyRow callers fall outside this kernel subset; their RMW should not be routed as arbitrary whole-value writes without preserving atomicity.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r1-yang-what-each-option-protects-with-a-shared-vk`>>

!!! What each option protects with a shared VK


(a) Owner tab seals: VK never leaves owner tab; owner can still open all vessel rows, so this does not cryptographically isolate faces. Writes stall if owner disappears unless authorized ownership transfers while preserving tab-only key custody. Current owner selection is the port that stands island (`shared-holder-core.ts:211-220`).

(a′) Asker seals: best causal attribution and tab-only key residency. But occupied-carrier validation requires open power. With one VK across faces, any eligible session carrying it can open/replace another face's carrier unless row-level face authorization or distinct face keys gate it. Sync arrival can wait by named `seal-awaits-session`; good fit to no-clock rule. Current router sends custody writes owner-only, so routing must change.

(b) Seal-only facet: current custody writer cannot express it. It accepts a full `VesselKey` and invokes open-under-VK for the old carrier (`sealed-writer.ts:43,208-218,279-309`). A pure encrypt/sign facet cannot prove an occupied row is its own without opening it. Append-only generation writes or an independent transition proof could change this. No split sealer/opener abstraction appears in this path.

(c) Tab-side sealed store: could isolate only if keys and rows split per face. A second store under the same VK adds no crypto boundary and conflicts with one Repo/one owner, adding two locks/stores and a merge problem.

Per-face keys: LEAD recommendation is to ask for distinct face archive/custody sealing keys if one face must never open another face's documents. Existing per-face content-key seat is for bag content, not the VK custody table (`sealed-store.mem:65-67`). Decide explicitly which rows are vessel-common (vessel archive/root) and face-private (veil archive) before changing the key hierarchy. Content keys alone do not solve Keyhive archive custody.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r1-yang-ranked-verdict`>>

!!! Ranked verdict


# (a′) Asker seals, conditional on row-to-face authorization or per-face keys. It keeps the VK in tabs and ties tab-caused writes to their cause; uncaused sync writes wait for an unlocked same-face session. Without that additional boundary it is only routing policy over a vessel-wide key.
# (a) Owner seals. Simple key residency and current routing shape, but owner lifetime becomes availability and it does not improve cross-face reach.
# (b) Seal-only facet. Not expressible with current occupied-carrier proof. Rank rises if append-only/new-generation storage or an independent authenticated ownership proof removes need for plaintext open.
# (c) Tab-side store. Rank rises only if independent per-face stores/keys are desired and a one-owner/Repo replication law is supplied.

What flips ranking: owner-only authority as an explicit product rule favors (a); a storage protocol that can validate generation ownership without decryption favors (b); settled independent face stores with distinct keys favors (c). (a′) loses if writes lack trustworthy face labels or admission/session identity.

Biggest probe: in one browser vessel, seal distinct A and B archives, unlock each in separate tabs, close the owner tab, then issue A-caused, B-caused, and sync-arrival replacement writes. Trace recipient tab and whether it can open the other face's existing carrier. Finally close the last tab and verify no VK/content keys remain and writes wait/refuse by named cause until re-unlock. This resolves routing, prior-row open, cross-face reach, and last-tab floor together.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r1-yang-house-constraints-read`>>

!!! House constraints read


MEASURED: per-tab unlock, face-scoped sessions, holder retains face content keys only while a same-face session is unlocked, and last-session close locks that face (`bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/session-unlock-2026.mem:25-40`). Key table distinguishes VK, content keys, Keyhive member keys, bundle key, and outbox key (`bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store.mem:65-72`). Browser SharedWorker owns one island and port/session binding (`packages/lararium-browser/src/shared-holder-core.ts:181-220`). No browser-platform compatibility claim is needed for this code verdict; no external platform source was used.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r1-yang-wave-two-twin-claim-audit-and-merged-view`>>

!!! Wave two: twin claim audit and merged view


YIN's twin handback is TWIN.md; its platform evidence is distinct from this code lens. Status tags below say whether a finding was supported independently by both handbacks, only by YANG's code pass, or only by YIN's platform/code pass. MEASURED and LEAD retain their original meanings.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r1-yang-twin-claim-audit`>>

!!!! Twin claim audit


* ''MEASURED — confirmed-by-both:'' SharedWorker should not be treated as a durable awake-tab detector or vault. YIN cites the HTML SharedWorker model, Chrome lifecycle/release documentation, MDN, and compatibility sources dated/accessed 2026-10-19. YANG's code pass independently finds explicit port/session bookkeeping but no liveness oracle (`shared-holder-core.ts:181-220`). Chrome Android SharedWorker support/reliability statements are time-sensitive and are dated in YIN's cited Chrome 148 release note (2026-05-05); do not generalize that Android status to other browsers.
* ''MEASURED — twin-alone:'' MessagePort has no dependable peer-death close notification; Web Locks and BroadcastChannel do not prove a tab can answer promptly. Sources in TWIN.md: MDN MessagePort.close (last modified 2025-09-18), HTML MessagePort, MDN Web Locks and BroadcastChannel, accessed 2026-10-19. YANG did not independently inspect these platform APIs.
* ''MEASURED — confirmed-by-both, qualified:'' Browser `custody:write` routes to the owner, and owner messages go to the selected owner port (`holder-routes.ts:23-31`; `shared-holder-core.ts:117-125`). YIN likewise found `holder-routes.ts:30`. Neither handback proves an end-to-end browser custody handler is absent outside the inspected scope; that absence remains ''LEAD''.
* ''MEASURED — YANG-alone:'' A current custody overwrite opens the occupied same carrier before replacement (`sealed-writer.ts:208-218,279-309`); writing is full-value replacement, not caller-level read-modify-write. YIN explicitly left this detail unverified. Browser `writeKeystoreRowHeld` passes the VK into that path (`browser-custody.ts:383-402`).
* ''MEASURED — confirmed-by-both, scoped:'' Browser custody persistence uses the vessel key, while a separate face content-key seat exists (`browser-custody.ts:386-402`; `operator-daemon-behavior.ts:1034-1068`; `sealed-store.mem:65-72`). Both handbacks conclude that session routing over a shared VK is not cryptographic face separation. The phrase “every sealed row” should remain qualified: the inventory distinguishes VK-sealed custody rows from content-key records, bundles, and outbox parcels.
* ''LEAD — twin-alone / still unsettled:'' YIN flags the exact node veil-row choice via `mountedFace` as unconfirmed. The requested browser and daemon files inspected here do not settle that node-specific path; inspect `open-node-vessel.ts` and its writer call chain before relying on the prompt's mountedFace claim.
* ''MEASURED/LEAD — confirmed-by-both with scope caveat:'' Browser owner route is measured. The exact custody message payload and “no handler anywhere” are not proven by the inspected browser files. Likewise, YIN's statement that node `sessionProof` signs the session-caused act is supported by `operator-daemon-behavior.ts:77-80,1041-1062`; the claimed roughly 20 `updateCustodyRow` call sites and their complete caller list were not independently counted here and remain ''LEAD''.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r1-yang-what-the-platform-lens-adds-to-this-code-verdict`>>

!!!! What the platform lens adds to this code verdict


''MEASURED — twin-alone:'' A tab may freeze, be discarded, or terminate while the SharedWorker and other tabs have different lifetimes; the worker's survival is not a contract that a sealing tab will answer. The browser design must treat an unanswered seal request as an explicit unavailable condition, not infer wakefulness from a port, Web Lock, BroadcastChannel presence, or wall-clock timeout. The specific platform claims and dates are recorded in TWIN.md and cited there: Chrome Energy Saver freezing guidance (2025-01-20), Chrome extended-lifetime SharedWorker trial (2025-07-31), Chrome 148 release notes (2026-05-05), and MDN/HTML pages accessed 2026-10-19.

''LEAD — inferred from measured platform behavior:'' (a′) must authenticate each port/session and bind the request to its admitted face; same-origin SharedWorker connection alone is not authorization. If no qualifying tab answers, keep/retry the named `seal-awaits-session` operation without using elapsed time as lock policy. Abrupt tab loss cannot be treated as a clean lock event; explicit lock and last-session removal must clear held face content keys, while process death naturally destroys in-memory secrets when the holder itself dies.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r1-yang-merged-ranked-verdict`>>

!!!! Merged ranked verdict


# ''(a′) Asker seals — preferred, conditional on face-key policy and a safe replacement protocol.'' A tab-caused write should go back to its admitted asking session; an uncaused sync/delegation write should target an admitted unlocked session of the named face or wait as `seal-awaits-session`. It best preserves per-tab unlock and avoids a holder-retained VK/facet. However, the existing occupied-carrier proof opens the previous row, so an eligible tab needs open power for that carrier. With a vessel-wide VK that means routing is not face-level cryptographic isolation. Add per-face wrapping/sealing keys for face-private carriers, and identify the vessel-wide rows that remain common. ''What flips it:'' the operator requires a write to finish with all tabs asleep, or face labels/session provenance cannot be trusted; a proven scoped seal-only capability could also supersede it.
# ''(a) Owner tab seals — compatible fallback.'' Existing browser routing is already owner-only, and the VK stays in a tab. It makes owner liveness the availability bottleneck and still does not isolate faces under one VK. ''What flips it upward:'' the owner is deliberately the only authority allowed to perform all vessel custody writes and writes may wait for that owner.
# ''(b) Seal-only facet — not expressible by the current occupied-carrier contract.'' The current writer receives a VK and opens the old row on replacement. A true encrypt/sign-only facet cannot perform that proof. It could rise if append-only/versioned carriers or a separately verifiable transition proof removes old-row opening, and a capability can be scoped by face, revoked at explicit lock, and safely admitted to the holder. It would constrain operation authority, but with a shared VK it still would not itself create face separation.
# ''(c) Tab-side sealed store — least supported by present architecture.'' It introduces another store and lock while the holder owns one Repo. Under the same VK it creates no cryptographic boundary. It rises only if separate per-face stores and keys are a product requirement and ownership/replication rules resolve the two-store consistency problem.

''Per-face key ruling:'' MEASURED: the VK is vessel-level in the key table and browser custody lookup; face content keys are a separate seat (`sealed-store.mem:65-72`; `browser-custody.ts:386-402`; `operator-daemon-behavior.ts:1034-1068`). LEAD recommendation: define vessel-common versus face-private custody rows and use distinct face wrapping/sealing keys for face-private rows. This addresses the fact that (a), (a′), and even a seal-only capability over a single VK are authority/routing limits, not cryptographic face boundaries. Existing content keys should not be presumed suitable for Keyhive archive custody without checking restore/recovery semantics.

''Single biggest probe:'' instrument one browser vessel with two faces A/B and separate unlocked tabs. Seal and then replace each face's occupied carrier while logging which admitted port receives each tab-caused write and each sync/delegation write; test whether A can open or substitute B's carrier and vice versa. Freeze, discard, and terminate the owner and asker independently, then close the last tab and verify held keys are gone and the waiting/refusal cause is named. This single probe resolves the key uncertainty: whether current write routing, overwrite proof, face-key boundaries, and browser lifecycle can jointly satisfy the operator's tab-unlock stories. No suites/builds were run as part of this research.

<<~/ahu>>

