<<~ ahu slot=`#/r2-yin-ratchet-audit-twin-s-top-claims-against-cited-lines-pages`>>

!!! Ratchet audit: twin's top claims against cited lines/pages


| Twin claim | Finding and why | Provenance |
| ''MEASURED:'' SharedWorker is not a durable awake-tab detector; browser lifecycle can freeze/discard tabs, and Android behavior is implementation/version-sensitive. | ''CONFIRM.'' HTML defines worker sharing and ports, not an execution guarantee. Chrome Page Lifecycle guidance and Chrome 148 notes (2026-05-05) document freezing/reliability caveats; these do not promise a worker or tab will answer. The code's explicit port/session bookkeeping (`shared-holder-core.ts:92-105,192-250`) has no liveness oracle. | confirmed-by-both (code confirms no oracle; YIN supplies platform evidence) |
| ''MEASURED:'' MessagePort has no dependable peer-death close event; Web Locks/BroadcastChannel cannot prove an awake sealer. | ''CONFIRM.'' HTML MessagePort event model and MDN `MessagePort.close()` describe local close/message events, not reliable notification that a remote page died. Web Locks serialize ownership; BroadcastChannel delivers messages but provides no authoritative roster. Silence cannot establish asleep versus dead. Sources accessed 2026-10-19: [[HTML MessagePort|https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/web-messaging.html#message-ports]], [[MDN close|https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/MessagePort/close]], [[MDN Web Locks|https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Web_Locks_API]], [[MDN BroadcastChannel|https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Broadcast_Channel_API]]. | twin-alone |
| ''MEASURED:'' Browser custody writes route to the owner, and owner messages reach the selected owner port. | ''CONFIRM.'' `holder-routes.ts:23-31` routes `custody:write` to owner; `shared-holder-core.ts:117-125` resolves owner-only dispatch. This establishes route behavior, not that no downstream adapter exists elsewhere. | confirmed-by-both |
| ''MEASURED:'' Occupied carrier replacement opens the prior carrier; it is full-value replacement, not caller-side read/merge. | ''CONFIRM.'' `sealed-writer.ts:208-218` checks/openUnderVk on the standing bytes; `:279-298` proves tree, opens old carrier, seals sidecar and opens it back before rename. Caller can still supply a complete archive value. | twin-alone (YIN round one had not checked the writer) |
| ''MEASURED:'' Browser custody uses one vessel VK, so routing over it is not cryptographic face isolation. | ''CONFIRM, scoped to the named browser custody carriers.'' `browser-custody.ts:54-68,386-402` stores one `unlocked.get(idbName)` and passes that VK to carrier writer; key table `sealed-store.mem:65-72` distinguishes VK from content keys. Do not generalize to every mesh/content record: the latter have their own keys. | confirmed-by-both |
| ''LEAD:'' Node Veil row selection may be vessel-wide via mountedFace. | ''REFUTE as established claim / leave unresolved.'' The cited browser helper indexes Veil rows by handle (`browser-keyhive-archive.ts:86-94`); node provider-based archive write (`operator-daemon-behavior.ts:1045-1049`) does not prove a mountedFace selector. Need inspect the exact node carrier writer chain before asserting either behavior. | confirmed-by-both that this is unsettled |
| ''MEASURED/LEAD:'' Current code has owner route; exact `custody:write` payload and exhaustive lack of a browser handler are not established. | ''CONFIRM.'' The route excerpt proves destination only. Neither handback establishes payload schema or an exhaustive registration/dispatch search. Keep those as LEAD. | confirmed-by-both |
| ''MEASURED/LEAD:'' Node has many custody row update callers; retain atomicity and don't route them as arbitrary full-row writes. | ''PARTLY CONFIRM.'' `browser-custody.ts:404-417` has an exclusive composite read/decision/write act; the cited operator daemon wires key operations (`operator-daemon-behavior.ts:1034-1068`). The approximate caller count/inventory was not verified in this audit. Preserve atomic acts as a design constraint; caller count remains LEAD. | twin-alone |

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r2-yin-twin-lens-added-to-yin-s-platform-and-prior-art-ground`>>

!!! Twin lens added to YIN's platform and prior-art ground


''MEASURED — confirmed-by-both:'' Existing browser flow grants the VK for the vessel `idbName`; the browser custody comment explicitly says custody rows seal under vessel key (`browser-custody.ts:1-9,54-68,386-402`). Existing one-slot-tree-per-vessel behavior is measured there; independent face trees are a target, not current behavior. Existing carrier replacement must open the old carrier, so F6's asker must have the correct row-class *open* key under the current protocol (`sealed-writer.ts:208-218,279-298`). A seal-only facet cannot satisfy this overwrite contract as written.

''LEAD — twin-alone, accepted:'' A shared worker port, Web Lock, or broadcast presence cannot establish a tab's wakefulness; unanswered asker writes need a named wait/refusal, not wall-clock lock policy. Bind each request to admitted session and face; same-origin access alone is not authority. Browser house behavior remains per-tab unlock, last-tab floor; node unlocks once per boot. Platform references and dates above plus [[HTML Shared Workers|https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/workers.html#shared-workers-and-the-sharedworker-interface]], [[Chrome Page Lifecycle|https://developer.chrome.com/blog/page-lifecycle-api]], [[Chrome freezing on Energy Saver (2025-01-20)|https://developer.chrome.com/blog/freezing-on-energy-saver]], [[Chrome 148 notes (2026-05-05)|https://developer.chrome.com/release-notes/148]], all accessed 2026-10-19.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r2-yin-q3-prior-art-keys-and-what-a-person-touches`>>

!!! Q3 — prior art, keys and what a person touches


| Product | Separation and per-identity action | What the analogy does and does not establish |
| ''1Password'' | ''MEASURED (accessed 2026-10-19):'' A new account/device sign-in uses account password plus that account's 34-character Secret Key; the Secret Key is locally retained after enrollment and can be found in the Emergency Kit. Multiple accounts can coexist. Current support says accounts with the same account password unlock together; with different passwords, unlock separately. A person enters an account password, not the Secret Key on every routine unlock. [[Multiple accounts|https://support.1password.com/multiple-accounts/]] (published ~2026-08); [[Secret Key|https://support.1password.com/understanding-account-key/]]; [[Security Design v0.4.7 (2024-06-25)|https://1password.com/files/1Password-White-Paper.pdf]]. | Closest precedent for account-specific roots. It also shows shared human unlock passwords can intentionally collapse app unlock boundaries. Vaults inside an account are not separate account unlocks. |
| ''Bitwarden'' | ''MEASURED (white paper 2025; accessed 2026-10-19):'' One master-password login opens personal User Symmetric Key; the user's RSA private key (under that user key) opens each authorized Organization Symmetric Key. Item keys are wrapped under the personal or organization key by ownership. The person does not type an organization password for each organization in the described design. [[Security white paper|https://bitwarden.com/pdf/help-bitwarden-security-white-paper.pdf]]; [[Organizations|https://bitwarden.com/help/about-organizations/]]. | Good precedent for distinct key domains and explicit public-key delivery. Organization keys are deliberately shared with authorized members, so not equivalent to a face-private key inaccessible to VK holders. |
| ''Signal Desktop / linked devices'' | ''MEASURED (Signal article 2025-01-27; support page accessed 2026-10-19):'' A new linked device displays a QR; a person authenticates on primary phone and scans/approves. The primary provisions account keys encrypted to the new device's generated key. Devices share identity key material but have device-specific message keys. Optional history transfer uses a one-time history-encryption key. [[Synchronized Start|https://signal.org/blog/a-synchronized-start-for-linked-devices/]]; [[Linked Devices|https://support.signal.org/hc/en-us/articles/360007320551-Linked-Devices]]; [[Sesame spec|https://signal.org/docs/specifications/sesame/]]. | This is one identity across devices, not several identities locally unlocked on one desktop. QR is an enrollment touch, not a per-tab unlock prompt. |
| ''Matrix / Element'' | ''MEASURED (Matrix spec v1.7; Element guide accessed 2026-10-19):'' Cross-signing is a per-user master/self-signing/user-signing trust hierarchy over devices/users. Secure Secret Storage encrypts private cross-signing and backup secrets; a new device is verified from an existing device or restored with recovery/security key or passphrase. [[Matrix Client-Server spec v1.7|https://spec.matrix.org/v1.7/client-server-api/]]; [[Element recovery key guide|https://docs.element.io/latest/element-support/device-verification/how-to-ensure-you-have-a-recovery-key/]]; [[Element security guide|https://docs.element.io/latest/element-support/matrix-account-management/securing-a-matrix-account/]]. | Again primarily per-account device trust/recovery, not a guarantee that multiple accounts in a client share or separate local unlock boundaries. Matrix spec describes keys; Element guide describes user recovery UX. |

''LEAD comparison:'' Lares's per-tab ask is stricter than these app-level examples: every tab asks its own face unlock; an enrolled tab's key material lives only in that session and last-tab close returns browser to floor. For face-only work, the face route should be sufficient; vessel route is needed only for VESSEL-COMMON acts. Avoid making every face operation demand both prompts if no common row is touched. For node, unlock VK once per boot and load each face only through its own face unlock/recovery authority.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r2-yin-q1-row-classes-unlock-shapes-restore-and-removal`>>

!!! Q1 — row classes, unlock shapes, restore and removal


''Current measurement boundary:'' `browser-custody.ts:1-9,54-68,386-402` documents the named browser rows and seals the custody set under one VK. `sealed-store.mem:65-72` names separate VK, member, content, bundle and outbox keys. The following row classification is a target recommendation; some carriers mix face and vessel material and need splitting before classification is implementable.

| Named custody material | Target class | Reason / caveat |
| Vessel Keyhive archive | ''VESSEL-COMMON only if truly floor/transport-only.'' Otherwise split its face identity/archive payload into one archive per PersonaGroup and mark each ''FACE-PRIVATE''. | Current archive is vessel-level (`operator-daemon-behavior.ts:1002-1033`; browser saves `open-browser-vessel.ts:678-679,732-737`). K15 requires one identity per PersonaGroup and none above. Do not call an archive common if it contains face authority. |
| Each `veil-archive-h<N>` | ''FACE-PRIVATE'' | Handle-indexed archive (`browser-keyhive-archive.ts:86-94`; node anchors `identity-anchors.ts:138-168`). |
| Face-key custody (`face-proof-key-h<N>`, `vessel-face-key…`) | ''FACE-PRIVATE'' | Scoped to one face; bundle selection is face-specific (`face-key-custody.ts:8-20`; `vault-carriers.ts:315-318,376-382`). |
| Guardian sets `guardian-set-h<N>` | ''FACE-PRIVATE'' | A face's guardian policy/recovery association; its guardian secrets remain with guardians. Bundle only that face's set (`guardian-enrolment.ts:1-18`; `vault-carriers.ts:334-338`). |
| Face-provisional | ''FACE-PRIVATE'' | Pending/restore confirmation for one face (`face-provisional.ts:1-18,55-70`; bundle selector `vault-carriers.ts:339-342`). |
| Fleet-join custody | ''FACE-PRIVATE'', with one exception: pending join protocol envelope is transient and must be bound to its target face. | Group-named address secret/record belongs to joining face (`fleet-join-custody.ts:4-20`; `vault-carriers.ts:319-331`). `fleet-join-pending` has one in-flight request and sensitive secret but lacks durable face identity (`fleet-join-custody.ts:9-11,91+; vault-carriers.ts:324-327`): redesign/bind to one face; until then it is a vessel-common transient exposure, never bundle it. |
| Handle book | ''Split:'' minimal routing index ''VESSEL-COMMON''; each face's labels/recognition/private entries ''FACE-PRIVATE''. | The recognizer book is a VK carrier; a selected face bundle copies only its recognized records (`browser-circle-store.ts:57-67`; `vault-carriers.ts:332-333`). A common whole-book encryption key would expose face-private entries. |
| Persona roster | ''VESSEL-COMMON'' opaque routing metadata; roots, identity material and content-bearing fields ''FACE-PRIVATE''. | Current roster is a hot VK row and lists roots/handles (`browser-custody.ts:60`; `vault-carriers.ts:305-306`). Minimize face labels/existence disclosure. |
| Slot tree | ''Two classes:'' VK tree ''VESSEL-COMMON''; independent face-X tree ''FACE-PRIVATE'' and opens only FCK-X. | Current tree is one `custody-slots` per vessel, VK lookup by `idbName` (`browser-custody.ts:54-68,386-402`). Never wrap a face tree/key under VK. |
| Device seed / `vessel-key` | ''VESSEL-COMMON, but never a face-key ancestor.'' | Device seed and public half are vessel key row and current slot tree wraps VK (`browser-custody.ts:5-9,298-306`; `vault-carriers.ts:287-290`). If K15 uses per-face device material, mint distinct face secrets independently; do not let the vessel seed derive/open FCKs. |

''Three shapes — what each browser tab types/touches (LEAD):''

# ''(i) Two independent slot trees — recommendation.'' For face-X-only action, tab supplies/touches X's passphrase/paper/approved PRF slot to open random FCK-X. If the same act needs common rows, separately unlock VK through vessel slot. Each tab performs its own unlock; credentials may coincidentally match but must remain independent trees/keys. Node opens VK once per boot for common operations; face X's own unlock is separately required for X-private authority. Recovery: X bundle contains X FCK recovery slots/material, identity/archive, guardian set and X rows only. Restore X without opening Y. Removing X revokes join/member admission and discards local X FCK/slots when its last session closes; VK and Y remain. Old X backups remain readable to holders of X recovery material until rotated/re-encrypted.
# ''(ii) derive FCK from face Keyhive secret.'' Tab first unlocks X's hive with X's face-specific route; derivation must use high-entropy private material, domain-separated and versioned, never public identity bytes. Human can do one face touch if that same touch opens the hive. Recovery requires X bundle to restore that secret plus KDF context before deriving FCK; removal/re-found identity can strand old rows unless migration rewraps them. Risk: archive/key bootstrap circularity; current K15 description does not establish a dedicated custody KDF secret.
# ''(iii) FCK wrapped to X's member key.'' Tab unlocks X's hive and uses X's private member key to unwrap FCK; one face touch only if hive unlock genuinely supplies that key. Restore requires X archive/member key and wrapped FCK plus X guardian route. Member revocation alone does not erase cached keys or old bundles; circularity arises if the member key can only be opened from the archive that FCK is supposed to protect.

''Joint invariant:'' In every design, FCK wrapped under VK is no isolation: any VK holder unwraps it. Isolation exists only if X's own unlock/recovery path can open X key while VK alone cannot. No wall-clock timeout is a lock event. Last tab/session close drops its held keys and rests browser at floor.

''Bundle and guardian rule (LEAD):'' face-X bundle is one-face: X identity/archive/event slice/content-key seat, X FCK plus recovery slots or wrapped form and enough X bootstrap material, X guardian set, X face-private provisional/join/face-key rows selected for restore. Guardian secrets stay with guardians; bundle carries X's authenticated recovery metadata, not Y's set or keys. Common bundle carries only VK recovery plus minimized opaque roster/routing data. Removing X revokes X future admissions and rekeys X's live group where that group protocol supports it; it cannot revoke already exported plaintext or old offline bundles. It must not rotate VK or make Y/common data unreadable.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r2-yin-reconciliation-shape-choice`>>

!!! Reconciliation: shape choice


''LEAD — Q1 ranked:'' (i) first, with independent per-face FCK slot trees; (iii) second only if Keyhive member-key bootstrap is proven non-circular and guardian recovery can restore independently; (ii) third unless K15 explicitly adds a stable secret root and lifecycle. YIN's wave-one preference for (iii) is revised after comparing twin's recovery analysis: member-key/archive circularity is a real architecture dependency, while independent slot recovery is direct and testable. (i) does not require two prompts for every act: face-only action asks X; common-row action additionally asks VK. Any common VK-readable payload containing face secrets invalidates the classification regardless of routing.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r2-yin-q2-placement-in-k15-and-composition-with-f6-slice-three-a`>>

!!! Q2 — placement in K15 and composition with F6 slice three (a′)


''MEASURED:'' K15 defines a Keyhive identity, store, registry, archive and content-key seat per PersonaGroup; K15 step 6 migrates identity bytes, split stores and archive format together (`keyhive-per-persona-group-2026.mem:55-72`; `stable-founding-road.mem:203,456`). The existing writer opens prior carrier on overwrite (`sealed-writer.ts:208-218,279-298`).

''LEAD — dependency/order:'' per-face custody keys belong *inside K15* because keys, archive/store ownership and one-face restore are all part of that PersonaGroup boundary. F6 slice-three (a′) is the write choreography layered onto the K15 row/key ownership; it cannot manufacture isolation over VK.

# ''K15.1 substrate proof:'' separate two-hive stores, registries, archives and key seats; interleave/reboot/replay and cross-read/mutate red probe. No FCK derived from process-global/shared state.
# ''Before bytes move, specify row classes and key hierarchy:'' VK slot for common; independent random FCK and own recovery slot tree per face; authenticated carrier-name/face/key-class binding, versioning and face-only bundle shape. Separate mixed carriers (archive, handle book, roster) where necessary.
# ''K15.1/.2 face contexts/identity ownership:'' face provider gets its own hive, event store, registry, archive, FCK and content seat; remove vessel identity delegation from face events. FCK never derives from VK. Preserve common VK only for floor metadata.
# ''K15.3 sessions + F6 slice three (a′):'' bind each tab session to one face and its independent unlock; caused write asks the admitted asker tab. Uncaused sync/delegation targets an unlocked admitted tab for that named face or waits as `seal-awaits-session`. Common writes target a VK-unlocked session. Because current replacement opens old row, the chosen sealer must hold that row class's open key. No wall-clock lock; last tab returns browser to floor; node VK once per boot plus per-face authority.
# ''K15.4 removal/content keys:'' keep FCK distinct from per-bag content keys; face removal rolls/revokes that face's group only. Carry guardian lifecycle and key rotation semantics.
# ''K15.5 one-face restore/herm:'' restore only X's identity/archive/events/key memory/FCK/guardian set; herm transports ciphertext without face capability. Restore X and prove Y absent/unopenable.
# ''K15.6 single migration fence:'' identity bytes + split stores + slot trees/FCK + archive/custody formats migrate together as one class change. Mixed legacy material refuses by name and follows re-found path; no silent cross-face import or “A now, B later.” F6 routing ships against this scoped format as one end state.

```text
K15.1 substrate proof
       ↓
row classes + independent FCK/slot/recovery + authenticated binding
       ↓
K15.1/.2 face contexts and identities
       ↓
K15.3 tab/session binding + F6 slice-three asker sealing (open with row-class key)
       ↓
K15.4 removal/content-key lifecycle → K15.5 one-face restore → K15.6 atomic migration fence
```

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r2-yin-final-ranked-recommendations-and-one-red-test`>>

!!! Final ranked recommendations and one red test


# ''Q1:'' choose ''(i)'', separate VK and random independent FCK slot trees. Class rows as above; split mixed rows. Per-tab face-only action touches that face's route; common operation additionally opens VK. Bundle/guardian restore contains exactly one face. Removal affects only that face and its old backups remain recoverable by holders of its recovery material.
# ''Q2:'' place FCK ownership and row partition in K15.1/.2, bind it to K15.3 and F6 slice three (a′), complete K15.4/.5, then migrate atomically at K15.6. F6 asker routing follows established key scope; it never substitutes for crypto separation.
# ''Single red test proving key isolation, not policy:'' create occupied A and B face-private carriers and distinct FCK/slot trees. Unlock only A; bypass router and try to open, replace and export B using every key available to A, ''including VK and A's Keyhive/member keys''. Require authenticated-open failure and byte-for-byte unchanged B. Same-face A replacement must succeed. Restore A's bundle on a fresh vessel and prove B rows/keys/identifiers are absent and cannot open; repeat for B. Any B open via A or VK is red. This directly tests the key boundary, not route denial.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r2-yang-yang-wave-two-ratchet-per-face-custody-keys`>>

!! YANG · Wave two ratchet: per-face custody keys


Research date: 2026-10-19. Repository source is read-only; no suites, builds, or git writes were run. Paths are relative to `/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine`. ''MEASURED'' = directly shown in source or cited vendor/spec page; ''LEAD'' = inference or recommendation. Each finding also identifies provenance: ''confirmed-by-both'', ''yours-alone'' (YANG code lens), or ''twin-alone'' (YIN platform lens). Round-one baseline: [[PRIOR-YANG.md|../PRIOR-YANG.md]], [[PRIOR-YIN.md|../PRIOR-YIN.md]]; ratchet handbacks: [[MINE.md|MINE.md]], [[TWIN.md|TWIN.md]].

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r2-yang-a-ratchet-audit-of-twin-md`>>

!!! A. Ratchet audit of TWIN.md


# ''MEASURED · confirmed-by-both:'' browser custody takes one VK from `unlocked.get(idbName)` and passes it into the sealed carrier path (`packages/lararium-browser/src/browser-custody.ts:386-402`); the sealed-store key table distinguishes vessel key and content keys (`bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store.mem:65-72`). This supports the narrow claim that the browser custody carriers here are vessel-key protected, not per-face protected. It does ''not'' establish that all stored ciphertext everywhere uses VK: sealed content records use their content-key / Keyhive lockbox hierarchy. TWIN’s caution against saying “every sealed row” without scope is correct.

# ''MEASURED · yours-alone:'' occupied-carrier replacement opens/proves the existing carrier under the supplied key before landing replacement (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/sealed-writer.ts:208-218,279-309`). This is not caller-side read-modify-write: browser callers can supply a whole replacement value. TWIN did not independently verify that code detail. Consequence: a separate encrypt-only facet cannot replace an existing carrier under the current contract.

# ''MEASURED · confirmed-by-both, scoped:'' `custody:write` is routed to the owner in the closed browser route table (`packages/lararium-browser/src/holder-routes.ts:23-31`), and owner delivery resolves to the selected port (`shared-holder-core.ts:117-125`). Neither handback proves that no adapter/handler exists anywhere else; “no handler anywhere” remains LEAD unless the full dispatch graph is inspected.

# ''MEASURED · twin-alone:'' SharedWorker execution/lifetime is not a promise that a tab remains awake or will service a request; HTML defines the worker interface, while Chrome lifecycle guidance describes frozen/discarded pages. MDN/HTML do not provide a dependable remote `MessagePort`-death signal; Web Locks/BroadcastChannel likewise do not certify responsiveness. Sources consulted 2026-10-19: [[HTML Shared workers|https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/workers.html#shared-workers-and-the-sharedworker-interface]], [[Chrome Page Lifecycle|https://developer.chrome.com/blog/page-lifecycle-api]], [[Chrome Energy Saver freezing (2025-01-20)|https://developer.chrome.com/blog/freezing-on-energy-saver]], [[Chrome 148 release notes (2026-05-05)|https://developer.chrome.com/release-notes/148]], [[MDN MessagePort.close|https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/MessagePort/close]], [[MDN Web Locks|https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Web_Locks_API]], [[MDN BroadcastChannel|https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Broadcast_Channel_API]]. YANG’s code pass did not independently audit those platform contracts.

# ''MEASURED · confirmed-by-both, qualified:'' holder tracks explicit ports/sessions/owner and clears owner on explicit `disconnect` (`shared-holder-core.ts:92-105,192-221`); no code finding shows peer process death automatically runs disconnect. Platform silence must not be read as proof of death/wakefulness. ''LEAD:'' no qualifying unlocked face session means a named wait/refusal such as `seal-awaits-session`, never a wall-clock lock or guessed liveness.

# ''LEAD · confirmed-by-both:'' with a shared VK, asker/owner routing is authority policy, not cryptographic face separation. A tab that gets VK can open VK-sealed carriers of other faces. Distinct FCKs must be cryptographically independent of VK; wrapping an FCK only under VK makes every VK holder an FCK holder.

# ''MEASURED · yours-alone, correction to TWIN’s unresolved census:'' node’s one `keyhive-archive.bin` row is vessel-wide and VK-hot (`packages/lararium-node/src/vault-carriers.ts:290-291`, `identity-anchors.ts:124-146`), while `veil-archive-h<N>.bin` is face-indexed but also VK-hot today (`vault-carriers.ts:292-293`; `identity-anchors.ts:138-168`). Browser `CUSTODY_ROW` likewise seals both archives and many face rows under one VK (`packages/lararium-browser/src/browser-custody.ts:20-27,386-402`). Face naming and bundle selection do not change current encryption keys.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r2-yang-b-twin-lens-applied-to-code-row-census-and-corrections`>>

!!! B. Twin lens applied to code: row census and corrections


The table below separates ''current code custody'' (the existing carrier class) from ''target cryptographic ownership''. “Hot/cold under VK” means current VK protection. The target scopes are LEAD design judgments; K15 can retire or split current “vessel” carriers. Carrier table is the authoritative node census (`packages/lararium-node/src/vault-carriers.ts:1-22,238-405`); browser has its own narrower IndexedDB row matcher (`browser-custody.ts:20-27`).

| Named material | Current code fact | Target class and design note |
| Vessel Keyhive archive | `keyhive-archive`, hot, VK (`vault-carriers.ts:290-291`); archive is a whole vessel identity archive (`identity-anchors.ts:124-146`). | ''VESSEL-COMMON only as a transitional/floor archive with no face capability.'' K15 says one identity/archive per PersonaGroup and no identity above them (`stable-founding-road.mem:203,456`); so any face-bearing archive must be split and is FACE-PRIVATE. Do not call a secret-bearing old archive “common” and leave it VK-openable. |
| Each `veil-archive-h<N>` | Per-face file; hot/VK and bundle semantics separate (`vault-carriers.ts:292-293`; `identity-anchors.ts:138-168`). | ''FACE-PRIVATE.'' Seal under FCK-N; bundle carries that face only. |
| Face-key custody | `face-proof-key-h<N>-…` is cold/VK and bundle whole; `vessel-face-key-…` is hot/VK and stays local (`vault-carriers.ts:313-318,379-382`). | ''FACE-PRIVATE.'' Face proof-key rows ride only face bundle when specified; local vessel face key remains bound to that face and is removed/rotated with that face. |
| Guardian sets | `guardian-set-h<N>` hot/VK, face-indexed, bundle whole (`vault-carriers.ts:334-338`). | ''FACE-PRIVATE.'' Guardian recovery set and its commitments must accompany the face’s recovery unit. Guardian-held secrets remain with guardians; only recovery material intended for local custody/bundle is present. |
| Face-provisional | hot/VK, face-indexed, no bundle (`vault-carriers.ts:339-342`). | ''FACE-PRIVATE.'' Local restore state for that face. |
| Fleet-join custody | Face address secret and join record are hot/VK, grouped by PersonaGroup, no face bundle; pending join is one hot/VK transient (`vault-carriers.ts:322-331`). | Address secret and join record ''FACE-PRIVATE''. Pending join is ''FACE-PRIVATE transient'', bound to the admitted target face; it must not cross-import. The twin’s “vessel-common transient” label is technically current carrier classification, but poor target security semantics: it contains face-specific X25519 request/secret and should be isolated or held under a short-lived face/join key. |
| Handle book | `.handle-book.json` hot/VK and bundle treatment `lines` (`vault-carriers.ts:332-333`). | ''FACE-PRIVATE partitions plus minimal common index.'' The code comment calls petnames/handle book private and says it closes the social graph at rest (`vault-carriers.ts:1-6`); one whole common-keyed book can leak cross-face relationships. Bundle `lines` already indicates face-selective export, not crypto separation. |
| Persona roster | Hot/VK, bundle `lines` (`vault-carriers.ts:303-309`). | ''VESSEL-COMMON routing metadata only'', with opaque IDs and minimal existence disclosure. Face roots/identity material must be private. If faces must not learn one another exist, partition/obfuscate roster too. |
| Slot tree | Node `vk-slots.bin` is floor/outside VK and wraps VK (`vault-carriers.ts:97-100,246-248`); browser has one `custody-slots` tree per vessel (`browser-custody.ts:20-27,54-68`). | Two classes: VK slots ''VESSEL-COMMON''; each face’s independently recovered FCK slot tree ''FACE-PRIVATE''. Do not wrap FCK under VK. |
| Device seed / vessel key | `vessel-key` plus `vk-slots` are floor-level vessel key material (`vault-carriers.ts:238-248`); browser binds device seed to VK tree (`browser-custody.ts:20-27`). | ''VESSEL-COMMON'', and never an ancestor/wrapper of FCK. Distinguish the vessel key from per-face device-share rows (`device-share` is face-indexed, cold/VK at `vault-carriers.ts:396`). |

''MEASURED · yours-alone:'' node’s `face` and `bundle` fields model ownership/export selection, not key class. All `hot` and `cold` entries are VK sealed in the carrier table’s class law (`vault-carriers.ts:1-22`), and the VK rotation derives its set from that table. Therefore, “face bundle carries only X” does not mean “X’s key is needed to read X at rest.” Browser storage is even clearer: all matched custody rows are sealed to the `idbName` VK (`browser-custody.ts:20-27,386-402`).

''MEASURED · yours-alone:'' key table already distinguishes VK, Keyhive member keys, content keys, bundle key and outbox key (`sealed-store.mem:65-72`). FCK must be a new explicit key class or intentionally defined wrapper domain; existing per-bag content keys are not an interchangeable custody key because they protect sealed content and have different seat/roll/restore lifecycles (`operator-daemon-behavior.ts:1034-1068`; `content-key-holder.ts:383-421`).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r2-yang-c-q1-shapes-per-tab-unlock-and-recovery`>>

!!! C. Q1 shapes, per-tab unlock and recovery


''LEAD · confirmed-by-both:'' select ''shape (i), made genuinely independent'': keep one VK slot tree for vessel-common records, and add a random FCK plus independent face slot/recovery tree for each PersonaGroup. A face slot opens only its FCK. It is never wrapped solely by VK. Shape (i) describes the simplest auditable human/recovery boundary; the FCK must also bind face ID, row name and format/version in authenticated associated data. Old-row overwrite opens with the same row-class key, as required by the measured writer behavior above.

* ''What Kalani or Leilani does per tab (LEAD):'' every browser tab performs its own face X unlock. If the tab needs only X-private work, it asks/touches X’s passphrase, paper route, or a measured device PRF slot and opens FCK-X. It does not unlock sibling faces. A vessel-common act additionally asks for the VK route in that tab, unless a distinct common session is already explicitly authorized; never treat having FCK-X as implicit VK authority. A node opens the VK once per boot per house law, but face-private operations still require the corresponding face’s own recovery/unlock admission. Last tab rests browser at floor; no idle timer.
* ''Recovery:'' a face-X bundle restores X’s identity/archive, FCK and its face slot recovery material, X’s guardian set/recovery references, X’s face-key custody, provisional state if policy requires it, and only X’s other bundle-selected rows. It contains no Y secrets/rows. Common VK slots/roster/opaque handle routing live in a separate vessel-common recovery unit. Guardian sets ride the relevant face bundle; guardian secret shares remain with their holders. A restore of X must succeed without ever opening Y. Keep one-face restore semantics already required by K15 step 5 (`stable-founding-road.mem:456`) and the carrier bundle selectors (`vault-carriers.ts:334-342,376-382`).
* ''Removal:'' explicit X removal revokes X admission/member authority, removes X local slot and FCK access and retires X face rows/bundle references; it does not rotate VK or Y’s FCK. Old copies of X’s bundle or cached plaintext remain usable to whoever retained X recovery material; removal cannot retroactively erase offline copies. K15’s fresh PersonaGroup/removal behavior applies to X alone (`stable-founding-road.mem:455-456`).

''LEAD · confirmed-by-both:'' shape (ii), deriving FCK from a face Keyhive identity secret, only works if that secret is itself a private, recoverable, stable unlock root and not public identity bytes or VK-encrypted data. It creates circularity if opening the archive is needed to recover the derivation input, and rotation/re-founding must preserve old-row migration.

''LEAD · confirmed-by-both:'' shape (iii), wrapping FCK to a face Keyhive member key, gives one face prompt only if the face’s private member key is already available through that face’s independent unlock. It can bind authority to membership, but creates bootstrap circularity if archive/member key is itself inside FCK-protected archive; removal requires rewrapping all active copies. Prefer only if K15 defines a non-circular identity key bootstrap. Neither (ii) nor (iii) should wrap FCK under VK.

''LEAD · yours-alone:'' the twin’s direct (i) choice is right, but its proposed “face restore bundle carries face slot tree” needs care: a recoverable slot tree may itself encode a usable credential wrapper. Put only the intended face recovery route/wrapped FCK in that face bundle, preserve secrecy of paper/passphrase/guardian secret shares, and test bundle import does not mint access to other face slot trees. Also separate target schema decision from current code: device seed is common, while per-face `recovery-device-share-h<N>` is face-private.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r2-yang-d-q2-k15-owns-the-key-data-boundary-f6-slice-three-consumes`>>

!!! D. Q2: K15 owns the key/data boundary; F6 slice three consumes it


''MEASURED · confirmed-by-both:'' K15 is the roadmap owner for one Keyhive identity, event store, registry, archive and content-key seat per PersonaGroup; its numbered order is explicit, and step 6 migrates identity bytes, split stores and archive format together (`stable-founding-road.mem:203,456`). F6 says slice three waits on its face context and lists owner-seals, scoped facet, or tab-side store as the current unresolved sealed-write shape (`stable-founding-road.mem:504`). Current occupied-carrier opening means (a′) needs the matching FCK or VK, not merely encrypt authority (`sealed-writer.ts:208-218,279-309`).

''LEAD · confirmed-by-both:'' cryptographic custody split belongs inside K15’s face context and step-6 migration fence, not as a route-only F6 patch. F6 slice three under (a′) is the write choreography after key class and row-to-face ownership are fixed.

''Dependency/order (LEAD):''

# ''K15.1:'' run the specified two-hive isolation probe and establish independent face contexts. In parallel before any bytes move, define the exhaustive row census, VESSEL-COMMON/FACE-PRIVATE schema, FCK class, independent slot/recovery tree, associated-data binding and legacy refusal rules.
# ''K15.1 completion / K15.2:'' each PersonaGroup owns its own hive/store/registry/archive and FCK domain. Remove vessel delegation from face-bearing events as K15.2 requires. Face archive bytes and custody rows cannot be reachable through an above-face identity or VK wrapper.
# ''K15.3 + F6 slice three:'' bind every tab/session to exactly one face and its own unlock. For tab-caused face writes, asker tab seals; for uncaused sync/delegation, target a currently unlocked tab for that named face or wait with a named cause. Common-row requests use a VK-authorized act. Maintain route/session authentication, and do not use timeout as a lock decision. The designated writer must open an existing carrier with its same-class key before replacement.
# ''K15.4:'' keep FCK distinct from per-bag content keys; implement per-face roll/removal, guardian lifecycle and no sibling rotation. A face removal cuts that face only.
# ''K15.5:'' implement one-face bundle/restore and herm carrying without face authoring; guardian recovery restores only that face.
# ''K15.6 + F6 end state:'' one migration fence moves identity bytes, split stores, per-face slots/FCKs, archive formats and custody row formats together. Reject mixed/ambiguous legacy archives by name; no “A now, B later” state. F6 slice three ships against the resulting row/key schema, not before it.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r2-yang-e-prior-art-check-four-systems-dated-2026-10-19`>>

!!! E. Prior art check: four systems, dated 2026-10-19


These systems are precedents, not evidence of Lares isolation. “Type per identity” distinguishes initial enrollment from every local unlock.

| System | MEASURED key boundary and human action | Provenance |
| ''1Password'' | Account data uses account password plus account-specific Secret Key in its published security design; new account/device setup needs both, while Secret Key is retained on enrolled devices and may be recovered from Emergency Kit. Multiple account contexts are supported; vaults under one account are not equivalent to separate account unlock roots. A person unlocking separate accounts types each account password when distinct; Secret Key is not retyped every unlock after enrollment. | ''MEASURED · twin-alone:'' [[Security Design white paper v0.4.7 (2024-06-25)|https://1password.com/files/1Password-White-Paper.pdf]], [[Secret Key support|https://support.1password.com/understanding-account-key/]], [[multiple accounts support|https://support.1password.com/multiple-accounts/]] (accessed 2026-10-19). ''LEAD:'' analogy is independent account unlock roots, not per-vault isolation. |
| ''Bitwarden'' | Personal items use User Symmetric Key; organization items use Organization Symmetric Key wrapped to each member’s RSA public key. One account login opens personal key and, if member, RSA private key/org key; organization does not normally demand a second password. Removal changes org membership/key access, not personal-vault key. | ''MEASURED · twin-alone:'' [[Security White Paper (2025)|https://bitwarden.com/pdf/help-bitwarden-security-white-paper.pdf]] (accessed 2026-10-19). ''LEAD:'' org keys are deliberately shared to members, unlike an isolated face key. |
| ''Signal Desktop linked devices'' | QR approval from authenticated primary provisions keys to a newly linked device. Devices are linked to the same Signal account; they share identity key pair while each has independent prekeys/session encryption keys. User touches/authenticates primary during linking; thereafter local device unlock, not one password per identity on desktop. This is one identity across devices, not several local identities. | ''MEASURED · twin-alone:'' [[Signal synchronized start (2025-01-27)|https://signal.org/blog/a-synchronized-start-for-linked-devices/]], [[Sesame specification|https://signal.org/docs/specifications/sesame/]], [[Signal linked device support|https://support.signal.org/hc/en-us/articles/360007320551-Linked-Devices]] (accessed 2026-10-19). |
| ''Matrix / Element'' | Cross-signing establishes user/device trust; Secure Secret Storage / recovery key protects account backup and cross-signing secrets. On a new device, the person verifies from an existing device or enters that account’s recovery key/passphrase. This is per-account recovery/device trust, not one password per device or a universal key across accounts. | ''MEASURED · twin-alone, updated with primary spec:'' [[Matrix Client-Server spec v1.7|https://spec.matrix.org/v1.7/client-server-api/]], [[Element recovery key guide|https://docs.element.io/latest/element-support/device-verification/how-to-ensure-you-have-a-recovery-key/]], [[Element security guide|https://docs.element.io/latest/element-support/matrix-account-management/securing-a-matrix-account/]] (accessed 2026-10-19). ''LEAD:'' closest analogy is account-scoped recovery plus device trust. |

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r2-yang-f-one-red-test-and-ranked-recommendation`>>

!!! F. One red test and ranked recommendation


''Single red test (LEAD · confirmed-by-both):'' create faces A and B with distinct random FCKs and independent slot/recovery trees, plus occupied veil archives and guardian/face-key/fleet carriers. Unlock only A; bypass every route check and attempt cryptographic open and replacement of B’s already occupied carriers using every key A receives, explicitly including VK, A’s member/identity secrets and common slots. Require authenticated-open failure and byte-for-byte unchanged B ciphertext. A can open and replace A. Restore A from A’s face bundle on a fresh vessel and prove B’s secrets/identifiers are absent and still unopened; repeat for B, then remove B and prove A and common rows remain usable. Any successful B open via VK/A material is red. This distinguishes key isolation from routing policy.

''Ranked final recommendation:''

# ''Q1: (i), independent FCK and face recovery slot tree.'' Vessel slot opens VK for common records; X’s per-tab ask opens only FCK-X. Face bundles are self-contained for exactly one face, with its guardian set; no VK-only wrapper, no sibling credentials. Classify carrier rows as above, and split private partitions out of common roster/book metadata.
# ''Q2: K15 owns the key boundary and data model.'' Define rows/FCK during K15.1, establish per-face contexts by K15.1–2, integrate asker-seals at K15.3/F6 slice three, finish per-face removal at K15.4 and one-face restore at K15.5, then migrate identity bytes, stores, key slots and archives atomically at K15.6. F6 depends on key ownership and occupied-carrier open authority.
# ''Red test:'' direct A-to-B authenticated-open and replacement attempt using all A and VK material against occupied B carriers, bypassing routing. It must fail cryptographically and preserve bytes; same-face replacement succeeds; one-face restore contains no sibling.

<<~/ahu>>

