<<~ ahu slot=`#/r3-yin-twin-lens-additions-to-my-prior-art-and-story-lens`>>

!!!! Twin-lens additions to my prior-art and story lens


''MEASURED (confirmed-by-both):'' Current browser name `custody-slots` and one VK are established, while independent per-face FCK trees remain target design (`browser-custody.ts:54-68,386-402`). Node's current floor/VK division does not make VK the parent of a face key by law. ''MEASURED (twin-alone):'' occupied carrier replacement authenticates the old ciphertext first (`sealed-writer.ts:208-218`); F6 implementation must keep atomicity and refusal semantics when it moves to per-face keys. ''LEAD (twin-alone):'' owner routing cannot prove liveness; the asker rule must bind the initiating admitted face/session, with named wait when no suitable live sealer answers. My platform review adds the important counterexample to a casual “device key solves it”: non-extractable WebCrypto is neither a device boundary nor user verification (MDN source below), and WebAuthn authorizes a scoped ceremony but doesn't decrypt app rows. (The routing additions don't overturn the no-browser-VK conclusion.)

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r3-yin-prior-art-system-versus-per-user-custody`>>

!!! Prior art: system versus per-user custody


All descriptions below carry ''MEASURED'', pages accessed 2026-10-19. These systems describe different OS boundaries, not requirements imposed on Lares.

| System | What a person types/touches for system-class acts | What stays protected before user unlock | Lares lesson |
| ''Android FBE / Direct Boot'' | DE data is available after boot, including Direct Boot, without a user unlock; CE becomes available only after that user unlocks. System DE can support narrowly scoped system work. [[AOSP File-Based Encryption|https://source.android.com/docs/security/features/encryption/file-based]], accessed 2026-10-19. | CE private user data remains unavailable. DE remains encrypted but is device/system accessible; AOSP says audit it and prefer CE, avoiding personal information in DE. | ''LEAD:'' a no-prompt device class can exist, but it carries weaker privacy than per-face custody. It does not justify placing identity or recovery material in common rows. |
| ''Apple Data Protection / keybags'' | Complete Protection uses a class key tied to passcode/password and device UID; unlock can use passcode or device biometric. Other classes support data while locked/after first auth. Apple silicon Mac draws its boundary at login/logout, not iPhone lock/unlock. [[Data Protection classes|https://support.apple.com/guide/security/secb010e978a/web]], published 2024-12-19; [[Keybags|https://support.apple.com/guide/security/sec6483d5760/web]], published 2024-12-19. | Class A can become inaccessible after lock; Class C remains accessible after first authentication; Class D on supported iOS-family devices is UID-only and affords limited protection. iOS/iPadOS/visionOS do ''not'' cryptographically separate filesystem contents per user; default iPhone/iPad device and user keybags coincide. | ''LEAD:'' classify rows by when/whose authority needs them. Apple is not evidence for arbitrary multiple face identities sharing device authority. |
| ''ChromeOS cryptohome'' | User signs in with key, usually password; cryptohome mounts then. No separate per-file prompt for system features already running in the user session. [[ChromiumOS User Sessions|https://www.chromium.org/chromium-os/developer-library/reference/user-sessions/user-sessions/]], accessed 2026-10-19. | Each user's unique vault is encrypted and unmounted at logout/reboot; system files are outside that user vault. [[Security whitepaper|https://www.chromium.org/chromium-os/developer-library/reference/security/security-whitepaper/]], accessed 2026-10-19. | ''LEAD:'' system work can occur outside private user storage; it need not open every user's private rows. |
| ''Windows DPAPI'' | Default `CryptProtectData` follows the same user's logon credential, normally with no second typed prompt while that context is active. `CRYPTPROTECT_LOCAL_MACHINE` allows any user on that computer to decrypt. [[Microsoft CryptProtectData|https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/desktop/api/Dpapi/nf-dpapi-cryptprotectdata]], accessed 2026-10-19. | User-scoped blobs require that account context; machine scope broadens access to local users. | ''LEAD:'' “device” authority is a broader principal; scope any such secret tightly and keep it from becoming an ancestor to face keys. |
| ''WebAuthn'' | No typed app secret is inherent. Authenticator may locally authorize key operation by touch, PIN/password or biometric. Its private-key operation and user verification occur inside authenticator boundary. [[W3C WebAuthn Level 3|https://www.w3.org/TR/webauthn-3/]], Recommendation dated 2026-08-25, accessed 2026-10-19. | Authenticator protects scoped credential operations; an assertion is not general local decryption of arbitrary Lares rows. | ''LEAD:'' a platform authenticator could act as a custody door, but would need a design tying it to actual row cryptography and recovery. |
| ''WebCrypto non-extractable key'' | Nothing is typed or touched by `extractable:false`; it forbids export/wrap via WebCrypto APIs. [[MDN CryptoKey.extractable|https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/CryptoKey/extractable]], accessed 2026-10-19. | It protects against API key export, not same-origin code using a live key; non-extractability alone doesn't promise hardware binding or user-verification. | ''LEAD:'' not a device unlock by itself. Don't relabel IndexedDB `CryptoKey` as hardware-held custody. |

''Synthesis (LEAD, confirmed-by-both):'' prior art distinguishes system/device operations from private per-user storage; it doesn't demand a human vessel password for browser system activity. A node-only boot custody door remains possible if a defined device-only confidential row survives partition. No surveyed precedent requires a browser vessel secret above per-face roots.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r3-yin-census-of-current-vessel-common-carriers-and-acts`>>

!!! Census of current vessel-common carriers and acts


The D/F/S label gives target disposition: ''D'' dissolve into independent per-face rows; ''F'' floor/public-by-design (no vessel key at floor); ''S'' retain a real vessel secret only for a demonstrated irreducible shared confidential payload. Where a row mixes classes, split first. Cause/frequency means semantic expected cadence, not telemetry.

| Act / current row(s) | Cause and cadence | Target sort and evidence |
| Founding: device seed / vessel key, VK and slot tree | Person, once per new vessel; repair can recur. | ''D+F'' for browser: face roots and FCKs independently random in their PersonaGroup; publish only opaque/public vessel routing facts. ''S'' only for a surviving node-only secret. Current browser root/tree/writer: `browser-custody.ts:2-12,54-68`; node floor classes: `vault-carriers.ts:239-259,287-290`. ''LEAD.'' |
| Fleet join offer, request, answer, land, abandon; pending row | Joining face/operator per join/rejoin; one pending request at a time; abandon only pending ceremony. | ''D'' pending secret, address secret and durable join record to the admitted target face. ''F'' signed/public offers and transport envelope. Node states one pending and X25519 secret: `fleet-join-custody.ts:4-20,95-110`; carriers `vault-carriers.ts:319-331`; browser `browser-fleet-join.ts:19-20,92-220`. ''MEASURED current, LEAD target.'' |
| Add/remove passphrase, paper or PRF slot | Face owner per enrollment/removal/recovery; occasional. PRF not enabled in current browser without measurement. | ''D'' each face's slot tree wraps only that face FCK. ''F'' any public slot metadata. ''S'' VK slots only if an actual irreducible common secret remains. Browser supports passphrase/paper and explicitly gates passkey/PRF: `browser-custody.ts:41-43,54-68`; slot APIs `:570-612`. ''MEASURED current, LEAD target.'' |
| Opaque roster / active face / petnames / declarations | Person when a face is founded/removed/renamed; occasional reads for selection. | ''D'' labels, roots, declarations and recognition records to face; ''F'' minimum opaque routing index if needed. Current rows: `vault-carriers.ts:301-314`, browser `browser-custody.ts:59-63`. ''LEAD:'' opaque does not mean harmless; minimize floor metadata. |
| Vessel Keyhive archive and face Veil archives | Initial founding plus archive saves/checkpoints; updates as identity changes. | ''D'' one Keyhive identity/archive per PersonaGroup, no identity above them; ''F'' only a truly public transport artifact. Browser currently names vessel archive and face archives in same custody matcher (`browser-custody.ts:5-8,59-60`); face archive indexing `browser-keyhive-archive.ts:81-94`; node writer selector unresolved above. ''LEAD'' target; selector audit unresolved. |
| Handle-book routing index / circle book | Recognizer writes when contacts/relations change; reads on route. Irregular. | ''D'' face-private labels/recognition/graph; ''F'' only minimal opaque routing index. Browser store says private memory of keys + labels: `browser-circle-store.ts:57-67`; node carrier `vault-carriers.ts:332-333`. Whole existing book should not become public. ''MEASURED payload characterization; LEAD split.'' |
| Device dyads, face/device edges, face proof/member keys, per-Nexus keys | Enrollment/join/reissue/revocation; verification reads on relevant operations. | ''D'' all face-specific private keys/relations; ''F'' signed public verification endpoints/claims where safe. Carrier face keys and bundle scope: `vault-carriers.ts:315-318,376-382`; `identity-anchors.ts:138-168`; browser wires face dyads `open-browser-vessel.ts:517,810-833`. ''LEAD target.'' |
| Herm's floor transport key / serving key | Node boot/raise and transport setup; usually per boot/read, rotates only on policy/compromise. | ''F'' public pin/certificate and public transport configuration. If serving key is actually secret, ''S'' only for that node's local custody domain, never a browser VK shared by faces. Node serving key currently separately wrapped under VK: `vault-carriers.ts:239-251`; browser reads floor card before face admission `open-browser-vessel.ts:220-225,752-769,835-843`. Need distinguish public pin from secret serving authority. ''MEASURED current, LEAD classification.'' |
| VK rotation / slot removal with rotation | Explicit operator action after route removal or compromise; uncommon. | ''D'' per-face rewraps if VK eliminated. ''S'' only if real common ciphertext remains. Current rotation opens/re-wraps census carriers `vault-carriers.ts:477-491`; browser unbind returns rotation offer and needs held VK `browser-custody.ts:594-606`. This current mechanism does not prove the common rows need that key. ''MEASURED current, LEAD target.'' |
| Face recovery / bundle restore / pending restore commit | Person on backup/restore or device loss; occasional per face. | ''D'' face-only bundle holds that face identity/archive/FCK/recovery/rows. ''F'' public routing inputs. ''S'' only if common recovery material truly remains. `face-bundle-restore.ts:200-240,338-390`; carriers `vault-carriers.ts:392-397`; custody law `bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody.mem:56,62,213-235`. ''LEAD target.'' |
| `tm-clear-password` / browser clear custody | Person deliberately asks to clear local custody; rare, explicit confirmation. | ''D'' clear each face route/rows separately; ''F'' public rows; remove VK only if no S row. Existing browser act opens and clears all sealed rows, then drops tree (`browser-custody.ts:613-650`); prompt explicitly names seed, archives, persona roots (`:613-625`). It must split under end-state design. ''MEASURED current, LEAD target.'' |

''Census result (LEAD, confirmed-by-both):'' none of these activities, as named, demonstrates a need for a browser-wide human-held vessel secret after mixed identity and face records split. Public transport/routing can sit at floor; private state partitions by PersonaGroup. A node may retain a boot-scoped secret only after naming a node-only secret payload. The words “device” or “vessel” in a carrier name do not establish that requirement. Floor has no vessel key in the end state.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r3-yin-re-asked-question-and-candidate-answer`>>

!!! Re-asked question and candidate answer


''The truer question:'' After each PersonaGroup owns its independently random FCK, slot tree, Keyhive identity/archive, fleet state and recovery bundle, and after floor rows expose only intentionally public routing/transport data, does any measured browser or node operation still require confidential bytes shared above the waking floor—and, if yes, which principal's custody door must open those bytes?

''Candidate answer (LEAD, confirmed-by-both):'' The census names no necessary browser-common secret. Browser end state has no VK: common operations either dissolve into face rows (''D'') or use public/floor data (''F''). Each tab asks for its own face unlock and receives only that face's FCK; any caused write routes by ruling (a′). The last tab drops face keys and rests at waking floor. Node follows the same per-face rule; its one-per-boot door opens only a specifically measured node-only secret, if any survives. If none survives, node has no common VK either. Thus the operator's A/B choice was framed around a secret the acts do not yet establish.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r3-yin-what-kalani-types-touches`>>

!!!! What Kalani types/touches


* ''One face on phone:'' one face credential or approved local face-unlock gesture for private actions; public floor operation asks nothing. No second vessel password. ''LEAD, confirmed-by-both.''
* ''Three faces on laptop:'' each face tab independently asks for that face's credential/approved authenticator. To use all three, unlock each face in its own tab; A's unlock never opens B/C. Public floor routing needs no credential. Final tab close returns to floor. ''LEAD, confirmed-by-both.''
* ''Node:'' no common credential if the D/F partition leaves no node-only secret. Otherwise operator uses the node custody door once per boot for that narrow system secret, and a face-private action separately requires that face's authority. ''LEAD, confirmed-by-both.''

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r3-yin-single-red-test`>>

!!!! Single red test


Create A, B and C with independent random FCKs, distinct slot trees, archives, fleet edges and recovery bundles. Put only public opaque routing/transport bytes at floor. Unlock A alone. Bypass all routing/session checks and try direct authenticated open, occupied-carrier replacement, restore and rotation of B/C using every key A received plus any floor/node-common key. Every B/C operation must fail cryptographically and leave ciphertext byte-for-byte unchanged; same-face A replacement must succeed. Run each census act while only A is unlocked: any act that requires A's key to operate on common data, or reads/writes B/C without B/C's own key, fails. Finally restore A alone to a fresh vessel and verify no B/C identity/key appears and floor can boot/read without VK. ''LEAD.''

<<~/ahu>>

