<<~ ahu slot=`#/r3-yang-1-census-each-currently-vessel-common-row-act`>>

!!! 1. Census — each currently vessel-common row act


''MEASURED:'' current browser custody matcher names `vessel-key`, `keyhive-archive`, veil archives, face roots, roster, nexus keys, face keys, join secrets, pending join and walks (`packages/lararium-browser/src/browser-custody.ts:54-68`). Bound rows are sealed under VK (`:10-12,386-402`). Node carrier definitions classify floor/hot/cold and identify face rows (`packages/lararium-node/src/vault-carriers.ts:239-259,287-398`). ''LEAD:'' D/F/S below gives target disposition, not current placement. D means split mixed carrier into independent face-owned rows. F means unsealed/public by design, each leaf starts at floor with its own root, and floor holds no vessel key. S is reserved for a named confidential cross-face system payload; the census has not demonstrated one.

| Act / current common material | Cause and cadence | Target | Evidence and boundary |
| Founding: mint device seed/VK, initial slot tree, archives, persona root/roster, charter/card | Person founding a vessel; once, with retry/repair if interrupted | ''D+F; S conditional'' | Browser founding persists seed/tree and archives (`open-browser-vessel.ts:700-745`; custody APIs `browser-custody.ts:298-306,524-612`). Node founds VK/SK (`custody-session.ts:80-100`); public card/charter are floor (`vault-carriers.ts:239-259`). Founding per-face root/archive D; signed card/charter and minimum opaque routes F. Only retain S if a distinct confidential system row is specified. |
| Fleet join: offer, request, answer, land, settle/abandon | Joining face and fleet, per join/rejoin; not normal boot | ''D'' pending, address secret, face key, records/certificates; ''F'' public signed carriage | Browser request and pending (`browser-fleet-join.ts:79-145`), landing/settlement (`:166-222`); node row definitions (`vault-carriers.ts:319-331`). Pending transient must bind to target PersonaGroup. Public offer/answer transport is not secret storage. |
| Add/remove passphrase, paper, or PRF slot; VK rotation after removal | Person per slot lifecycle, uncommon | ''D'' independent face trees and FCK; ''F'' public slot metadata as appropriate; ''S only conditionally'' | Browser slot kinds (`browser-custody.ts:41-43`) and bind/unbind (`:570-612`); node slot management/rotation (`custody-session.ts:80-100,267-329`; `vault-carriers.ts:477-510`). Current tree is vessel-wide. Under ruling 2, face route mutates only its face tree; no sibling-wide rotation. PRF only after device derivation is measured. |
| Opaque persona roster, active face, petnames, declarations, handles; add/remove face | Face founding/removal or operator selection; lifecycle and occasional selection | ''D'' labels, roots, declarations, private recognition; ''F'' minimal opaque routing index | Browser roster codec stores root indexes (`browser-custody.ts:313-325`); node rows (`vault-carriers.ts:301-314`); browser founding around `open-browser-vessel.ts:700-745`. Keep one Keyhive identity per PersonaGroup and none above them. Face existence/labels should not leak through a “minimal” index. |
| Keyhive archive / veil archive | Founding and each archive checkpoint/save | ''D'' each face's archive; ''F'' only if a remainder is a genuinely public transport artifact | Browser saves veil and Keyhive archives (`open-browser-vessel.ts:729-737`); node archive/anchor rows (`identity-anchors.ts:124-168`, `vault-carriers.ts:290-293`). No identity-bearing vessel archive survives K15. |
| Handle-book routing index | Peer/discovery/relationship updates and route reads | ''D'' face graph, labels and private relations; ''F'' minimal opaque routing facts | Node single `.handle-book.json` row (`vault-carriers.ts:332-333`); browser circle carrier cited in prior handback (`browser-circle-store.ts:57-67`). Split before declaring all common. |
| Device dyads, face/device edge, face proof/member keys, per-Nexus keys | Join/enrolment and relation change; reads during boot/verification | ''D'' private key and relation authority; ''F'' signed public endpoint/claim | Browser dyad and relationship call sites (`open-browser-vessel.ts:517,810-843`); node face/key carriers (`vault-carriers.ts:315-318,376-397`). “Device” does not make face authority vessel-common. |
| Herm floor transport key / serving key / transport configuration | Node raise/boot and transport setup; generally each boot, key rotation when explicitly required | ''F'' public pin/card/config; ''S only if'' secret serving authority really must be available before face unlock, scoped to node system custody | Floor rows and serving-tree distinction (`vault-carriers.ts:239-259`). Browser reads floor card/pins before face admission (`open-browser-vessel.ts:752-769,835-843`). Do not call a public pin a secret or bury a secret serving key in browser VK by analogy. |
| VK rotation (including removal with rotate) | Explicit slot-removal/recovery lifecycle; uncommon | ''D'' per-face rewrap; ''F'' floor excluded; ''S'' only if remaining named S carriers | Node rotation enumerates and reseals VK carriers (`vault-carriers.ts:477-510`; `custody-session.ts:267-329`); current implementation is broad, not proof of need. |
| Face bundle cut, recovery, restore, pending restore commit | Person recovering/moving one face; occasional | ''D'' all face bundle and restore rows; ''F'' required public routing inputs; ''S'' only for truly common secret | Node selectors and bundle semantics (`vault-carriers.ts:230-231,392-397`); restore preflight/commit (`face-bundle-restore.ts:200-240,338-390`); mesh bundle law (`bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody.mem:56,62,213-235`). Restore one face without opening another. |
| `tm-clear-password` / browser clear-custody | Person explicitly clears custody; rare and destructive | ''D'' each face separately; ''F'' public rows; remove only any genuinely necessary S | Confirm door and no-write refusal (`browser-custody.ts:34-39,613-665`); command dispatch (`open-browser-vessel.ts:1436`). This is not routine unlock. |

''Census result — LEAD, confirmed-by-both:'' no listed act by itself establishes a browser-wide shared secret. Founding, identity, join, roster, dyad, archive and recovery data dissolve into per-face roots (D); intentionally public/signed bootstrap and routing material belongs at floor (F). A node may retain an S class only after naming a secret-only system function and proving it cannot move to D or F. Its “once per boot” policy describes cadence, not necessity. No wall clock decides routing; browser asks each tab; last tab returns browser to floor; node unlocks once per boot if and only if S exists.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r3-yang-2-prior-art-system-versus-per-user-keys`>>

!!! 2. Prior art: system versus per-user keys


Sources opened/accessed ''2026-10-19''. These systems show useful splits, not requirements for Lares.

| System | What the person types/authorizes for system work | Protection while no user is logged in/unlocked |
| ''Android File-Based Encryption (FBE)'' | Direct Boot / Device-Encrypted (DE) data is available without entering the user's credential after boot. Credential-Encrypted (CE) data requires that user's PIN/password/pattern unlock. ''MEASURED:'' [[AOSP FBE|https://source.android.com/docs/security/features/encryption/file-based]], [[Android 15 CDD|https://source.android.com/docs/compatibility/15/android-15-cdd]] (accessed 2026-10-19). | CE user data remains unavailable until unlock. DE is deliberately available to Direct-Boot-aware software, so it must hold only data needed in that state; Android also separates system DE and per-user DE. |
| ''Apple Data Protection / keybags'' | Complete Protection classes need passcode or an allowed biometric unlock; less restrictive classes can remain available after first unlock or while locked, depending class. macOS login/logout boundary differs from iPhone/iPad lock. ''MEASURED:'' [[Data Protection classes|https://support.apple.com/en-sg/guide/security/secb010e978a/web]], [[keybags|https://support.apple.com/en-sg/guide/security/sec6483d5760/web]] (published 2024-12-19, accessed 2026-10-19). | Complete-protection class keys become inaccessible under their lock policy, while device-protected classes remain available. Caution: iOS/iPadOS/visionOS do not cryptographically separate filesystem contents per user; not a direct model for multiple local faces. |
| ''ChromeOS cryptohome'' | User signs in with account credentials; cryptohome mounts transparently. System processes outside a user profile do not demand a second per-file password. ''MEASURED:'' [[ChromeOS security whitepaper|https://www.chromium.org/chromium-os/developer-library/reference/security/security-whitepaper/]], [[user sessions|https://www.chromium.org/chromium-os/developer-library/reference/user-sessions/user-sessions/]] (accessed 2026-10-19). | Each user has a separate encrypted vault/keyset, locked on logout or reboot; logged-out cached data remains encrypted. System-only operation continues outside that user vault. |
| ''Windows DPAPI'' | Ordinary DPAPI use is bound to current user credentials/context and usually same machine, generally with no separate prompt if already logged in. `CRYPTPROTECT_LOCAL_MACHINE` allows any user on the computer to decrypt. ''MEASURED:'' [[Microsoft CryptProtectData|https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/dpapi/nf-dpapi-cryptprotectdata]] (accessed 2026-10-19). | User-scope data is protected from another ordinary user; machine-scope data is available to any machine user. Neither implies protection from a privileged/admin process. |
| ''WebAuthn / device-bound key operation and non-extractable WebCrypto'' | WebAuthn authenticator may authorize with touch, PIN/password, or biometric locally; no site secret need be typed. ''MEASURED:'' [[W3C WebAuthn Level 3|https://www.w3.org/TR/webauthn-3/]] (accessed 2026-10-19). | WebAuthn keeps authenticator private-key operations local and returns an assertion; it does not automatically encrypt arbitrary app storage. WebCrypto `extractable:false` blocks export/wrap through that API ([[MDN CryptoKey|https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/CryptoKey/extractable]], accessed 2026-10-19), but is not necessarily hardware-bound and same-origin code may invoke the key while usable. Thus it can provide a device-held unlock/touch, not a free system data class. |

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r3-yang-3-the-re-asked-question-and-candidate-end-state`>>

!!! 3. The re-asked question and candidate end state


''Re-asked question — LEAD, confirmed-by-both:'' “After each face-bearing row has an independent root and each floor-readable item is public by design, what confidential cross-face data remains, and which custody boundary needs to unlock it?” This unasks “how do vessel-common acts unlock?” because the census classifies acts by their rows; the verb common to multiple faces does not itself require a common secret.

''Candidate answer — LEAD, confirmed-by-both:'' Browser holds no VK. Dissolve all confidential identity/join/roster/dyad/archive/recovery records to per-face roots; leave only intentionally public opaque routing/transport at floor. Each admitted tab unlocks only that face's independent random FCK using that face's own slot tree. No FCK is wrapped by VK. If future node requirements identify a genuinely secret system-only payload that must survive before face unlock, give the node a separately named device custody root and open it once per boot; keep it out of browser and all face roots. If no such payload survives D/F, node has no common key either. A floor holds no vessel key. One Keyhive identity per PersonaGroup, none above them.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r3-yang-what-kalani-types`>>

!!!! What Kalani types


* ''One face on phone:'' one face credential/touch to open its FCK in that tab. Public floor operations ask nothing. No second VK prompt.
* ''Three faces on laptop:'' each face tab asks for its own face credential/touch: three independent asks to unlock all three (or per-face authenticator gestures). No one face opens another.
* ''Node:'' if a named secret S class remains, one device custody-door authorization once per boot; a face-private act also needs that face's admission/FCK. If all rows resolve D/F, no common credential at boot.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r3-yang-roadmap-landing`>>

!!!! Roadmap landing


* ''K15 step 3:'' land an independently random FCK for each PersonaGroup, with only its own slot tree able to open it; keep FCK out of VK. Partition mixed carriers and enforce one identity per PersonaGroup.
* ''F6 slice three:'' seal the write at its asker; route it to that admitted session's tab, else an admitted unlocked tab of the same face, else park it as `seal-awaits-session`. An unawake tab is not inferred from a timer or worker roster. A replacement must satisfy the standing carrier open contract.
* ''B1:'' no browser vessel secret; per-face rows under FCK and public floor rows only. Node S remains conditional on an explicit system-only secret inventory; node unlock once per boot if present.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r3-yang-single-red-test`>>

!!!! Single red test


Create faces A, B, C with independent random FCKs, distinct slot trees, face archives, join pending/secrets, dyads, guardian rows, handle-book partitions and restore bundles; put only public opaque routing/transport data at floor. Unlock A alone. Bypass routers and directly try authenticated open, overwrite, VK/face-key rotation, backup and restore of every occupied B carrier using every byte/key A receives and every floor/node-common value. ''Every B open must fail and preserve ciphertext byte-for-byte; same-face A replacement succeeds.'' Then restore A alone on a fresh browser: it gains no B identifiers/key/archive, and public floor work runs with no key. Invoke each census act with A admitted/unlocked and B locked; any operation that succeeds in reading or writing B, or demands A's key for a genuinely public/common row, fails the test. Test the F6 routing rule separately by issuing from A's admitted session: the write reaches that session, falls back only to another admitted unlocked A tab, otherwise waits under `seal-awaits-session`.

<<~/ahu>>

