<<~ ahu slot=`#/r4-yin-yin-lens-applied-to-node-side-types-what-wave-one-missed`>>

!!!! YIN lens applied to node-side types (what wave one missed)


''MEASURED (twin-alone):'' Node daemon begins with an empty in-memory custody holder (`packages/lararium-node/src/main.ts:336-345,371-374`); lock clears held VK/SK (`custody-holder.ts:151-155`). Herm has no face slots. The private place signing seed nevertheless remains available from a clear-at-rest file and supports floor signing (`node-vessel-identity.ts:429-442,451-487`). This is the principal counterexample to the blanket browser-derived statement “floor only holds public data.”

''LEAD (confirmed-by-both):'' Hearth is a composition over the node floor (`main.ts:353-374`; `vessel-caps.mem:139-143`), not a new key principal. It inherits the node's D/F/S partition and still needs per-face admission for face rows. A Herm's need to serve before a face exists is a real timing constraint: it cannot seat a face, and its raise carries ephemeral caps on a recognized operator (`vessel-standing.ts:60-79,113-119`). No current serving S row solves that need; the place signer is the distinct existing floor secret.

''LEAD (twin-alone):'' Docker `harbor`, `civic`, and `mesh-scenarios` package the node rather than adding a vessel secret. State may persist across restart while volatile face/serving keys must not; unattended restart may restore only agreed floor behavior. A humanless container cannot type the one-per-boot custody unlock, so any private D/S service pauses. Do not inject credentials through environment, image layers, health checks, or orchestration. Sources: `stable-founding-road.mem:56,66-68`; `tools/lares-container.sh` at roadmap `:68`; `founding-runbook.mem:156`.

''LEAD (twin-alone):'' CLI/MCP are seats against the daemon, not vessels with a second copy of VK/FCK. Each run/session proves its face; restarting an MCP client must not inherit another session's authority (`stable-founding-road.mem:201`; CLI door citations above). A node's boot unlock, if required, is distinct from that face proof.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r4-yin-2-locked-law-including-the-one-unresolved-house-conflict`>>

!!! 2. Locked law, including the one unresolved house conflict

//Frame, 2026-10-22: the operator has since ruled this conflict, so no reader takes it as open. `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road` #/rulings holds the answers: 2026-10-19 (//a shared act never makes a shared secret//, rows sorted D/F/S, with X0's scoped place signer) and 2026-10-20 (the place signer's fifth duty). The testimony below keeps its words.//


''LEAD — lock the D/F partition:'' A shared act never creates a shared secret by itself. Split every mixed carrier first: every confidential row attributable to a PersonaGroup is ''D'', sealed under that group's own independent random FCK and opened only by that group's own slot tree; no VK wraps an FCK. Only deliberately public/bootstrap/transport/routing bytes are ''F''. Retain ''S'' only for a named confidential system payload that cannot be dissolved into a face row and must act before any face exists; name its principal and opener. Each browser tab asks for its face independently; a node opens any approved node-only S once per boot; CLI/MCP sessions each present face authority. The last browser tab discards live FCKs and leaves floor; node shutdown/restart drops volatile face and serving keys. No wall clock picks an owner or expires a session. One Keyhive identity per PersonaGroup, none above them; asker seals.

''MEASURED conflict:'' The agreed phrase “a floor holds no vessel key” cannot be applied literally to current node code. It stores a private *vessel signing seed* at floor and loads it without a human at boot. This is not the custody VK (the slot-tree key that wraps mixed carriers), but it is still a vessel private key and cannot truthfully be called public F. The serving key (SK) is a third key, wrapped by VK; no serving row currently exists. ''House ruling needed to close the lock:'' either (1) amend “floor holds no vessel key” to “floor holds no custody VK/FCK; a separately named floor signer S may exist,” and explicitly accept/protect the current signer-at-rest behavior; or (2) require this signer S to live behind a named boot custody mechanism, which changes Herm's no-human-before-face startup behavior and must state how public relay service stands before authorization. Until that ruling, the shared D/F/FCK/asker law locks, but “every floor is public-only” does not. TWIN's suggested “explicitly accept clear-at-rest” option still needs the operator's express choice; it is not silently inferred from the prior agreement.

<<~/ahu>>

