| ''Node daemon packaged inside container'' | Same node rows; place signing seed remains separate current S exception. | Daemon boot currently auto-loads place signer; face/serving volatile keys start absent. Container restart clears those volatile keys. | Distinguish daemon restart from CLI/MCP client restart. | Restart with state mount: place identity/public floor only; no face/SK authority restored; every private operation refuses until relevant face/node door. |

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r4-yin-platform-vendor-spec-facts-that-constrain-the-law`>>

!!!! Platform/vendor/spec facts that constrain the law


* ''MEASURED (yours-alone):'' AOSP says CE storage is available only after user unlock and DE is available during Direct Boot; it recommends CE where possible. This proves the OS supports a system/user storage split, not that Lares should put a vessel key in DE. [[AOSP File-Based Encryption|https://source.android.com/docs/security/features/encryption/file-based]], accessed 2026-10-19.
* ''MEASURED (yours-alone):'' Apple says iOS background extension pages are nonpersistent, may unload, and must save/restore state from storage. This supports re-opening after lifecycle loss, not assuming a live worker as a custody holder. [[Apple Safari extension guidance|https://developer.apple.com/documentation/safariservices/optimizing-your-web-extension-for-safari?changes=_5]], accessed 2026-10-19.
* ''MEASURED (confirmed-by-both):'' Web Locks coordinate same-origin scripts/workers; lock ownership is coordination, not proof of an awake tab or authorization to open a face. [[MDN Web Locks API|https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Web_Locks_API]], accessed 2026-10-19.
* ''MEASURED (confirmed-by-both):'' WebAuthn Level 3 describes scoped public-key credentials and authenticator ceremonies; touch/PIN is not generic decryption of arbitrary IndexedDB. Bind any authenticator result cryptographically to the face FCK route before calling it an unlock. [[W3C WebAuthn Level 3|https://www.w3.org/TR/webauthn-3/]], Recommendation dated 2026-08-25, accessed 2026-10-19.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r4-yin-4-roadmap-landing`>>

!!! 4. Roadmap landing


''MEASURED (confirmed-by-both):'' K15 owns one identity/store/registry/archive/content-key seat per PersonaGroup; step 6 moves identity bytes, split stores and archive format together (`stable-founding-road.mem:203,456`; `keyhive-per-persona-group-2026.mem:55-72`). F6 slice three moves Repo/kernel into browser holder, routes sealed writes to asker, and lands with K15 step 3 so each face's custody rows seal under its own key (`stable-founding-road.mem:504-507`). B1 is the browser one-face/custody work referenced by prior handback (`../PRIOR-YIN.md` and `../PRIOR-YANG.md`, 2026-10-19); the exact B1 source lines were not re-opened in this pass, so treat that label as inherited rather than newly measured.

''LEAD sequencing:'' K15.1 define exhaustive row schema and independent FCK/slot/recovery tree; K15.2 enforce per-face identity ownership and split mixed carriers; K15.3 bind browser tab/session and node session to one face, apply (a′), land F6 slice three with authenticated occupied-row replacement; K15.4 remove one face and its rows without sibling rotation; K15.5 one-face recovery/restore; K15.6 migrate identity bytes/stores/FCK routes/archive in one fence. F6 routes writes over established key ownership; it cannot create isolation over VK. Keep Herm's private place signer decision and container no-human restart behavior explicit prerequisites. `sealed-writer.ts:208-218,279-309` shows current occupied-row replacement authenticates existing ciphertext and verifies before rename; preserve that refusal/atomicity in the face writer.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r4-yin-lock-witness`>>

!!!! Lock witness


For every vessel, instantiate faces A/B/C with independent random FCKs, distinct slot trees, archives, joins, dyads, handle-book partitions and recovery bundles; put only intended public bytes at F. Unlock A alone, bypass session/router checks, and try to decrypt, overwrite, rotate, export, back up and restore every B row using all A and floor-held bytes. B must always fail with ciphertext unchanged; same-face A replacement must pass. Then perform each row-bearing act and prove it touches only its owner or F. End by killing sessions/restarting: browser leaves F; node loses volatile face/SK keys; herm has only whichever separately ruled place signer and public floor. For tablet, unattended private D transform must fail. For containers, unattended restart must refuse D/S. For guest, a hostile boundary must not read either sibling face. For CLI/MCP, forged or restarted sessions must not retain authority. These are intended red tests, not executed.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r4-yin-5-open-evidence-ruling-items`>>

!!! 5. Open evidence/ruling items


# ''Critical ruling:'' approve the private place signing seed as a separately named floor S (then amend “floor holds no vessel key” precisely), or move it behind a named custody door and accept that a herm's no-face public service changes. Current code is clear-file and auto-load; neither handback's browser finding nor the node SK settles this.
# Current serving key has no serving row; future serving class is conditional and must not be used to justify current VK unlock.
# Prior census leaves node Keyhive archive row selector unresolved; prove row selector before migration.
# Exact native-shell custody and guest-owned persistent store are not measured; treat the latter as separate LarVessel if it appears.
# Tablet unattended sync supports F/ciphertext only under current law. Any demand for private D work requires a separately approved system S or work shifted to node.
# No suites, builds, device experiments, or proposed red tests ran in this read-only research round.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r4-yang-yang-wave-three-node-side-confirmation-and-lock`>>

!! YANG — wave-three node-side confirmation and lock


Research date: ''2026-10-19''. Repository `/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine` was read-only; no suites, builds, or git writes were run. This handback is the complete wave-three note. ''MEASURED'' means direct source/code evidence. ''LEAD'' means classification or target design. Paths and line numbers below refer to the repository snapshot read today. `../PRIOR-YIN.md` and `../PRIOR-YANG.md` were read first; their shared D/F/S census is the row inventory used here.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r4-yang-ruling-what-the-herm-serving-key-is`>>

!!! Ruling: what the herm serving key is


''MEASURED:'' Node founding currently mints a vessel key (VK), serving key (SK), and custody root; SK is wrapped by the VK entry in `sk-slots.bin`. Unlock opens VK then SK and holds both; lock drops them. `packages/lararium-node/src/custody-session.ts:80-116,154-190,227-242`; `packages/lararium-node/src/custody-holder.ts:151-155`. The carrier table says the serving class is for rows sealed under SK, but explicitly reports that ''no serving row stands yet'': `packages/lararium-node/src/vault-carriers.ts:12-14,249-251`.

''MEASURED:'' A Herm has no seated PersonaGroup: `personaSlotCeiling("herm")` is unconditionally zero, and its raise carries a recognized operator's caps on that operator's keys, with no persistence across reboot: `packages/lararium-mesh/src/vessel-standing.ts:60-79,113-119`; `bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/vessel-caps.mem:143`; `bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/waking-floor.mem:200-202`. Herm boot composes a place DID, public/replicated crossroads and read/relay faces; the raise door is a separate ephemeral recognition path: `packages/lararium-node/src/open-node-vessel.ts:3242-3258,3264-3291`; `packages/lararium-node/src/main.ts:558-575,604-614`. No current Herm caller opens SK for its raise or read-face.

''Verdict — LEAD, with a material exception to the earlier handback's broad “no node S” candidate:'' SK is a *secret key by nature*, but it is ''not a current Herm S row'', because there is no serving payload for it to protect and no Herm call site that consumes it. It cannot be made F: the canon says future serving rows are confidential, independently sealed under SK, with a serving gate distinct from both floor and sovereign gates (`bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody.mem:27,40-49`; `bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road.mem:188,214`). Nor should SK be replaced by the Herm's place key: place identity and serving-row custody have distinct scopes. If a future serving row lands, it creates a genuine node-local S class and requires the row's own serving authorization.

''MEASURED exception:'' The node already has a distinct private *place signing seed* (`vesselSeed`), read from a clear keypair file at boot. It signs as the vessel/place and derives public-floor gate children; it is explicitly distinct from the PersonaGroup root. `packages/lararium-node/src/node-vessel-identity.ts:296-314,340-348,429-487,491-499`; carrier table calls `vessel-key` a floor row (`vault-carriers.ts:239-248`); Herm composition receives the seed at `open-node-vessel.ts:3283`. Its public half/card/inception are F, but the private seed itself is confidential and not F. ''LEAD classification: this place key is an existing node-only S'', opened by the daemon at each boot without a human typing or touching anything today. It must be named separately from VK: current usage proves it cannot simply be erased or moved into a face FCK. This is exactly where the abstract law needs a node-specific clause: “floor has no VK” remains true, but “floor is only public material” is false for current node code. To meet the agreed custody law, either protect this S under a narrowly named node boot custody mechanism, or explicitly accept and document the present clear-at-rest place-seed exception; do not disguise it as public F. Thus the node has S today even though no serving S row is measured. The code's SK is redundant for current Herm behavior and must not be conflated with this place-key S.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r4-yang-locked-law`>>

!!! Locked law


''LEAD — lock (reconciled):'' Every private record attributable to a PersonaGroup dissolves into that face's rows (D), sealed by that face's independently random FCK and opened only by that face's own slot tree. A floor contains intentionally public/bootstrap/routing material (F), never a VK; any private node place key is separately named S, never relabeled F. A vessel may have an S set only where an inventoried confidential cross-face/system payload cannot be dissolved into D or made public by design; name each row, its owning principal, and its boot-opening mechanism. Browser tabs ask separately per face; CLI and MCP calls present their own face/session at the node gate. Current node daemon opens its place signing seed automatically at boot (an existing, unresolved S exception); any VK-protected node S additionally requires the node's once-per-boot custody unlock. No clock chooses an owner or expires an unlock. The final browser tab leaves browser floor only; node shutdown/restart drops volatile face and serving keys and returns to floor, while persistent place S is available again to the next boot under current code.

The two prior handbacks' current-row census remains authoritative: founding/roster/identity/archive/handle-book/dyad/join/recovery/rotation/clear-custody are mixed current carriers, but the private pieces are D and the deliberately public pieces F; no browser S was found. Their file:line evidence and D/F/S assignments are in `../PRIOR-YIN.md` sections “Census of current vessel-common carriers and acts” and `../PRIOR-YANG.md` section “Census — each currently vessel-common row act.” Node carrier classes are measured at `packages/lararium-node/src/vault-carriers.ts:239-398`; those current placements do not establish target necessity.

<<~/ahu>>

