<<~ ahu slot=`#/r5-yin-wave1-q3-how-always-on-identities-are-protected`>>

!!! Q3 — how always-on identities are protected


| System | MEASURED, 2026-10-19 | Boot consequence and Lares mapping (LEAD) |
| ''systemd credentials'' | `LoadCredentialEncrypted=` accepts a credential encrypted by `systemd-creds`; credentials may bind to TPM2, host key, or both. With persistent `/var` and TPM2, default encryption combines them so the credential is tied to local hardware and OS installation. Plain `SetCredential=` is unsuitable for private keys because unit files are world-readable. Sources: [[systemd credential documentation|https://systemd.io/CREDENTIALS/]], [[systemd-creds manual|https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/257/systemd-creds.html]], [[systemd credential implementation docs|https://github.com/systemd/systemd/blob/main/docs/CREDENTIALS.md]]. | This encrypts the file credential at rest, then systemd decrypts and delivers it to service at boot. No person is required if machine key/TPM policy allows release. Disk theft alone is reduced; compromise of an accepted boot/service context can still expose the credential. Local key + TPM binding is the closest off-the-shelf analogue to candidate (1), but TPM auto-release is still unattended authorization, not a human unlock. |
| ''TPM2-sealed key'' | systemd-creds can use TPM2 keying and PCR policy. PCR binding gates release against measured boot state; a signed PCR policy can authorize selected boot measurements. Sources above. | Machine releases only under configured boot state; a human PIN can add presence, but is incompatible with unattended release. TPM sealing does not protect against a hostile authorized running OS that can ask TPM for the key. Lares can use hardware protection as an optional wrapper, not as proof of operator presence. |
| ''HashiCorp Vault auto-unseal'' | Vault supports delegated auto-unseal using cloud KMS/HSM; on startup Vault connects to delegate with an encrypted root key for decryption. Instance identity/permissions authorize KMS access. Source: [[Vault integrated storage deployment guide|https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/tutorials/day-one-raft/raft-deployment-guide]], [[Vault HSM partners|https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/docs/partners/hsm]]. | No human shares are entered at each start; delegated machine identity performs unseal. This shifts trust to the KMS/HSM and host identity. It is availability-oriented delegated custody, not unattended public-only service. It maps to a boot door only if the house explicitly accepts a node machine principal. |
| ''Kubernetes Secrets'' | Secret objects are base64-encoded and, by default, stored unencrypted in etcd. Encryption at rest is separately configured; local encryption key on control-plane host does not protect against host compromise. KMS envelope encryption keeps KEK outside Kubernetes. Pod creation rights can indirectly expose namespace secrets. Sources: [[Secrets|https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/secret/]], [[encryption at rest|https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/encrypt-data/]], [[good practices|https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/secrets-good-practices/]]. | Kubernetes delivery to a pod is not itself a safe custody boundary. At rest depends on configured provider; at runtime an authorized pod can read the mounted value. A mounted signer credential can be exposed through pod/host/admin compromise. Do not treat orchestrator secret injection as an automatic Lares human unlock. |
| ''Tor onion-service identity'' | Tor v3 keeps a private master Ed25519 key in the service directory; it is the stable service identity and derives blinded signing keys. Tor spec explicitly labels the master key long-term and distinguishes online descriptor signing keys; spec notes offline master-key possibility. Sources: [[directory format|https://tpo.pages.torproject.net/core/torspec/rend-spec/fs-contents.html]], [[v3 protocol overview|https://spec.torproject.org/rend-spec/protocol-overview.html]]. | Conventional host persists key locally and starts unattended; a copy can impersonate the onion identity. Offline master-key use improves separation but requires a delegated operational flow. This is the closest identity analogy: a service key establishes public place identity; it does not imply authority to open unrelated user custody. |
| ''WireGuard'' | WireGuard model configures private keys for interfaces and associates peer public keys with allowed IP routing; the paper illustrates reading private key from configuration. Source: [[WireGuard paper|https://www.wireguard.com/papers/wireguard.pdf]]. | A long-lived host key enables unattended tunnel identity; protecting the config/host is operator responsibility. Theft lets attacker impersonate that peer and claim configured routes. It is network admission, not evidence for Lares face data access. |
| ''OpenSSH host keys'' | `sshd_config`'s `HostKey` names private host keys; sshd refuses key files that are group/world accessible. Source: [[OpenBSD sshd_config|https://man.openbsd.org/sshd_config.5]]. | File permissions protect against ordinary local users, not disk/root theft. sshd reads keys and starts without a person; compromise enables host impersonation. This supports a narrow named place signer protected by ownership/permissions, but not calling it public data. |
| ''Let's Encrypt / ACME'' | ACME account is associated with a key; Let’s Encrypt account ID can be rediscovered by registering with the same key. CAA docs note account key as a barrier to a domain hijacker issuing malicious certificates. Sources: [[account IDs|https://letsencrypt.org/docs/account-id/]], [[CAA|https://letsencrypt.org/docs/caa/]]. | ACME clients persist account keys and renew unattended; theft allows attacker to act as that account, subject to ACME/domain validation controls. A compromised ACME account is revocable/replacable, but this is not by itself a KEL recovery mechanism. |
| ''KERI / KERIpy'' | KERI establishment events publish current signing keys and commit by digest to next keys. Rotation needs current signatures and opens committed next keys; next private keys are the recovery boundary. KERI docs state pre-rotation helps mitigate later current-key compromise; if root pre-rotated keys are captured, no KERI-internal recovery exists. Sources: [[KERI 1.0 spec|https://trustoverip.github.io/kswg-keri-specification/]], [[KID0005 commentary|https://identity.foundation/keri/kids/kid0005Comment.html]], [[vLEI KERI infrastructure governance|https://www.gleif.org/media/pages/organizational-identity/become-a-vlei-issuer-qvi/vlei-ecosystem-governance-framework/23f7b8278d-1774607226/2025-04-16_vlei-egf-v3.0-technical-requirements-part-1-keri-infrastructure-2024_v1.3_final.pdf]]. KERIpy's keeper model has current/next public lots and rotation indexing ([[keripy keeping.py|https://github.com/WebOfTrust/keripy/blob/main/src/keri/app/keeping.py]]). | Pre-rotation key should be kept offline or under stronger threshold custody; its exposure is much more serious than current online signer exposure. A human need not be present for ordinary current-key signing, but recovery rotation needs access to next key(s). Pre-rotation cannot prevent forgery while stolen current key remains authoritative; it supplies verifiable path to supersede it. |
| ''Signal'' | Signal's server-side key transparency service maintains append-only, cryptographically protected key mappings and proofs to make service tampering detectable; it is distinct from end-to-end user identity keys. Sources: [[Signal Automatic Key Verification|https://signal.org/blog/automatic-key-verification/]], [[Signal key-transparency server|https://github.com/signalapp/key-transparency-server]]. Signal protocol publishes user identity keys/prekeys to server; the server does not thereby receive the user's private identity key ([[PQXDH spec|https://signal.org/docs/specifications/pqxdh/]]). | Transparency/consistency proofs help detect equivocation; they do not make the server's own host signing key inaccessible or create a human boot ceremony. Analogy: public floor artifacts can be witnessed/transparency-checked; per-face private keys remain separate and client-held. |

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r5-yin-wave1-q3-synthesis-lead`>>

!!!! Q3 synthesis (LEAD)


At-rest protection claims differ. `chmod 0600` / owner-only file (OpenSSH-style) protects against other unprivileged local users. TPM/systemd machine binding protects more strongly against copied disks and wrong boot states, while still auto-releasing under the approved host. Cloud KMS/HSM auto-unseal removes the key-encryption key from local disk but grants service identity a machine-authorized decrypt path. Kubernetes Secret API storage alone promises no at-rest encryption by default. Tor, WireGuard, and ACME examples show the common operational reality: identity keys are persistent and automatically loaded for availability, so compromise of the host's effective runtime identity compromises its public service identity. KERI adds independently committed future control, while Signal transparency supplies detection of inconsistent public mappings.

None of those analogies means a place signer should open a face's custody row. The place identity's authority must remain typed and scoped to public place acts. A hardware root that silently auto-unseals a signer changes the threat from “file readable” to “machine identity can request use”; it does not make the operator present.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r5-yin-wave1-q4-third-shape`>>

!!! Q4 — third shape


''LEAD recommendation: split a long-lived place root from a boot signer, and give the place identifier a KERI-like pre-rotated recovery path.'' The online signer may sign a tightly enumerated set of place acts and public floor-gate derivations. An offline owner-held root or threshold-held next key authorizes rotation of the online signer and publishes a verifiable KEL event. Keep at least one next key outside the always-on node. Use TPM/systemd encrypted credential as an optional at-rest wrapper for the online signer on supported nodes, with explicit fallback policy on systems lacking TPM.

This third shape improves on plain clear-file candidate (1) by making stale online-key theft recoverable and machine-tied at rest where available. It improves on human-at-every-boot candidate (2) by preserving Herm public service and unattended container restart. It does ''not'' erase the theft window: attacker with current signer can forge until verifiers accept the rotation; attacker with next/root key can seize or permanently lose the place identity. If recovery cannot be implemented with independently protected next key, do not claim pre-rotation protection. Root/next compromise in pure KERI can require abandoning the identifier and re-founding identity with ecosystem-level rebinding.

An alternative is candidate (1) strictly bounded and wrapped by owner-only file / OS keystore: simple and compatible, but a disk reader with the file gets the place identity. Candidate (2) can protect a sensitive private serving payload if there is one, but current evidence says no serving row exists, and gating the place signer would interrupt unattended floor identity. Do not conflate the two secrets.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r5-yin-wave1-q2-if-attacker-reads-the-current-seed-at-rest`>>

!!! Q2 — if attacker reads the current seed at rest


''MEASURED from round-four code audit, 2026-10-19:'' the node privately reads `signingKey` from local key file (`packages/lararium-node/src/node-vessel-identity.ts:429-442`), uses it for floor signing/device capability (`:451-487,491-499`), and supplies it to Herm (`packages/lararium-node/src/open-node-vessel.ts:3283`). It is independent of VK and SK; `packages/lararium-node/src/vault-carriers.ts:239-251` places `vessel-key` and `sk-slots` at floor. The prior handbacks report no current serving row/SK consumer. Herm has zero PersonaGroup slots and raises with recognized operator caps (`vessel-standing.ts:60-79,113-119`; `open-node-vessel.ts:558-614`).

''LEAD threat model:'' a seed reader can impersonate the place/vessel wherever verifiers accept its public key, forge any act signed by that key, and derive/sign any child floor-gate keys that the implementation derives from it. They can forge a place card/device identity, KEL inception or rotation only where that seed is the controlling KEL signer, and any dial/gate proof or carry-for seal using it. Exact cryptographic domains and callers need Q1's source audit; the handback names place acts and floor-gate derivation, not a verified exhaustive caller list. They cannot mathematically derive VK, a face FCK, a PersonaGroup identity root, or decrypt a face's ciphertext solely from this seed. A forged place admission/proof could still cause service-level deception if a relying gate trusts place signature as face authorization; design must ensure it does not. It cannot open custody rows unless code incorrectly uses place signer as a wrapper/opener, which current round-four evidence says it does not.

Detection: compare KEL histories / witnessed public floor records, keep tamper-evident receipts and independent public-key pins, and alert on conflicting valid histories or unexpected rotation. Recovery: rotate place key through authenticated KEL pre-rotation if next key remains safe; revoke/replace relying pins and re-found/rebind if recovery authority is also lost. Kapae/re-found semantics are house-specific and were not independently verified in this internet pass, so this mapping is ''LEAD'', not a measured procedure. A stolen current key enables forgery until rotation is accepted; pre-rotation does not retroactively erase that window.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r5-yin-wave1-q1-bounded-mapping-from-the-round-four-canon`>>

!!! Q1 — bounded mapping from the round-four canon


''MEASURED (round-four audit, dated 2026-10-19):'' private `vesselSeed`/`vessel-key` seed is clear at rest and auto-loaded at node boot without a person; it is not VK or SK. Public floor identity/card material is distinct from the seed. The seed signs as vessel/place and derives public floor-gate children, per the caller/line summary in the prompt: `node-vessel-identity.ts:296-314,340-348,429-499`; `vault-carriers.ts:239-248`; `open-node-vessel.ts:3283`.

''MEASURED scope caveat:'' I have not freshly opened source in the read-only repo for an exhaustive caller/domain inventory. The handoff specifically names place acts, floor-gate child keys, place DID, KEL inception, vessel card, carry-for seals, dial and gate proofs as candidate signing/derivation sites. Treat this enumeration as user-provided audit context, not independently confirmed completeness. Public relay/card/gate-floor identity must be available before a face exists for Herm boot, node restart, and waking floor if it stands in that role. Face-private access and face-authored acts can wait for their face admission. Whether all listed proofs must be emitted at boot vs on demand is a code-call-graph question unresolved here.

''LEAD:'' named floor signer should never sign a face act or open custody; KEL inception/rotation authority is a special high-impact place identity operation and should likely require owner/next-key ceremony rather than ordinary boot signer. Derived children need domain separation and a public manifest naming exactly which derivations are permitted; otherwise “place acts only” is not an enforceable boundary.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r5-yin-wave1-ranked-recommendation-and-per-vessel-effect`>>

!!! Ranked recommendation and per-vessel effect


# ''Third shape: named place signer + separately protected offline/threshold place root and pre-rotated next key; optionally TPM/systemd-wrap online signer.'' Best blend for unattended public availability and recoverability. It still recognizes an online signer as a limited place authority. Herm/node can start at floor; a compromised online seed is revocable only while next/root authority is safe.
# ''Candidate (1): named floor signer protected at rest by owner-only file, OS keystore where available, ideally machine-bound encrypted credential.'' Operationally simple and supports herm/container/waking-floor unattended startup. Disk seed theft gives place impersonation; face custody stays cryptographically separate. Must explicitly name signer S and allow no VK/FCK at floor.
# ''Candidate (2): human boot custody door.'' Strongest human-presence gate for whatever key it gates, but makes Herm's public place identity wait for an operator and breaks unattended container restart/waking-floor identity unless service remains deliberately anonymous/public-key cached. Use only for a genuinely sensitive node serving S, not automatically for this public place signer.

| Vessel | Effect under ranked third shape (LEAD) |
| Herm | TPM/systemd or owner-only protected online place signer auto-available; public card/relay/gate floor starts without face; no face rows. Offline next/root required to rotate. |
| Full node | Same place signer behavior; VK/FCK remain absent at floor. Any genuinely private serving S has its own boot door and remains locked without authorized unlock. |
| Hearth | Composition inherits node place identity, not extra key; every face's D rows remain individually admitted. |
| Container | Unattended restart resumes public floor if host-bound credential releases; no credential injected from image/env/log; unsupported host falls back to owner-only file or public-only mode. |
| Waking floor | Carries public identity/ciphertext and may verify proofs; unattended private-row decryption/transformation still needs a face or separately ruled S. |

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r5-yin-wave1-red-test-lead`>>

!!!! Red test (LEAD)


Build one end-to-end adversarial test around a copied data disk and a no-human reboot: (a) on approved machine/boot, public place card and public relay start; every exact place signer domain is logged against an allowlist; (b) neither `vesselSeed` nor derived private child bytes appear in disk image, environment, logs, API, or public artifacts; (c) a different host cannot unwrap the credential; (d) no VK/FCK is present and direct attempts to open, replace, restore, rotate, or decrypt any face row fail byte-preservingly; (e) same-host restart does not admit a face; (f) attack-simulate theft of the current place signer and show an independently held pre-rotated key emits a valid KEL rotation, after which old-key place acts are rejected; (g) compromise of the offline next key is treated as identity-loss/re-found, not claimed recoverable. For candidate (2), add the expected deliberate tradeoff assertion: no person means no signer and no signed place identity at boot.

<<~/ahu>>

