<<~ ahu slot=`#/r5-yang-wave1-q1-what-the-place-signer-can-do-today`>>

!!! Q1 — what the place signer can do today

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r5-yang-wave1-key-file-and-identity-lifecycle`>>

!!!! Key file and identity lifecycle


* ''MEASURED — 2026-10-19:'' `packages/lararium-node/src/node-vessel-identity.ts:140-170,185-198,429-448` stores the Ed25519 secret seed as clear hex JSON in a runtime-store-sibling identity directory, file mode `0600`; the seed is explicitly described as local and never synced. `loadVesselSigningSeed()` reads it, checks the seed derives the named public key, verifies the adjacent KEL inception, and returns a fresh 32-byte seed. This is filesystem permission protection, not encryption, hardware sealing, or an interactive door.
* ''MEASURED — 2026-10-19:'' `generateOrLoadVesselIdentity()` can mint only within an open custody claim and writes the key and inception together; `loadVesselIdentity()` is load-only. Normal daemon boot loads both public identity and private seed at `open-node-vessel.ts:945-946`, with no human unlock on that path. The identity is the place/vessel Ed25519 key, distinct in code and purpose from custody VK/SK and PersonaGroup root.
* ''MEASURED — 2026-10-19:'' The signer is also listed as `vessel-key` at floor in `packages/lararium-node/src/vault-carriers.ts:239-248`; `open-node-vessel.ts:3283` passes the seed into Herm composition. Therefore the earlier blanket wording “floor holds no vessel key” conflicts with current code. More precisely: floor has no *custody VK/FCK*, but currently has this private place signer seed.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r5-yang-wave1-direct-seed-consumers-and-signing-derivation-purposes`>>

!!!! Direct seed consumers and signing/derivation purposes


* ''MEASURED — 2026-10-19:'' `node-vessel-identity.ts:312` signs the place KEL inception when founding. `packages/lararium-mesh/src/vessel-kel.ts:2-13,35-59` fixes the signed domain to `vessel-kel`, with an `icp` event containing one public key and its self-signature.
* ''MEASURED — 2026-10-19:'' `packages/lararium-node/src/commands/nexus-contract.ts:250-268` uses the seed to sign `nexus carry-for`'s place carrier contract and, when a Nexus is present, the place floor-pin statement. These are place claims, not face signatures.
* ''MEASURED — 2026-10-19:'' `open-node-vessel.ts:1217` signs vessel-origin `askSeatDuties` asks; `:3087,3103,3115` signs node-island memory asks (`cut`, `door`, `import`). These signer calls authenticate this device/place at internal node verbs.
* ''MEASURED — 2026-10-19:'' `open-node-vessel.ts:1450` constructs a `carriageSignerFromSeed` used by `vesselCarriagePull`; its herm path signs place-floor carriage presentations and statements. The same region `:1394` derives relay-gate seed from this seed unless an explicit relay seed config overrides it. `carriage-relay.ts:61-76` HMAC-SHA256 domain-separates this transport gate from the Ed25519 place key; it does not reuse the raw key bytes.
* ''MEASURED — 2026-10-19:'' `open-node-vessel.ts:2279-2280` uses the seed as the device key signer and as `groupSecretOpenerFromSeed` for the sibling channel. `:2150` supplies it to daemon auth (`seed`). This means the seed is not merely a public-floor announcer key: node code uses it for device and group-secret cryptographic operations too. Mesh `persona-group-secret.ts:192` defines the opener; exact data exposure from a stolen seed depends on the sealed group-secret records and does not establish general access to face rows.
* ''MEASURED — 2026-10-19:'' `open-node-vessel.ts:3283` provides the seed to Herm as `signerSeed`; Herm has no PersonaGroup slot, but it does need this identity for its place-facing floor functions.
* ''MEASURED — 2026-10-19:'' `packages/lararium-node/src/commands/fleet-join.ts:285+` loads the place seed for a joining-device request signature. `packages/lares-cli/src/commands/meme.ts:1117` supplies a seed-backed signer to promote-check flow. `packages/lararium-node/src/commands/init.ts:246-251,320-327,491-510` uses it during founding/admit and place composition. These are explicit CLI/founding acts, generally operator-invoked, rather than unattended boot duties.
* ''MEASURED — 2026-10-19:'' `packages/lararium-mesh/src/dyad.ts:399-402` derives a founder veil keypair from `vesselSeed` and a group tag using hardened persona derivation. `open-node-vessel.ts:2150` hands the seed into daemon identity composition. Those derived identities are purpose-separated keys, but compromise of the root seed allows deterministic derivation where the derivation inputs/tags are available.
* ''MEASURED — 2026-10-19:'' `packages/lararium-node/src/main.ts:765-769` uses vessel identity as the publisher on a floor card. The public card itself is a derived/public artifact; the signing seed does not need to appear in the card.
* ''MEASURED — 2026-10-19:'' Floor-gate children do ''not'' expose the root seed. `node-vessel-identity.ts:454-499` implements `vesselFloorKeyDoor`: it re-reads and proves the key file for each registered domain, derives HKDF-SHA256 with empty salt and domain-as-info, clears its local seed buffer, and returns a fresh child. It refuses unregistered domains and the sovereign gate-bundle domain. `hearth-sovereign-pin.ts:56` and `herm-carriage.ts:64` derive floor gate bundles through that door. Thus a thief of the root seed can reproduce all registered floor children, but callers are intentionally denied the raw seed via this door.
* ''MEASURED — 2026-10-19:'' The CLI `loadVesselCard()` only reads cached ContactCard JSON (`node-vessel-identity.ts:375-393`); card persistence is mode `0600` (`:359-372`). The card is not freshly signed with the seed at each boot. The seed may have participated in founding Keyhive identity creation; the cached card is public-facing identity material, not proof of at-rest seed protection.
* ''MEASURED — 2026-10-19:'' `rg` across non-test source in the three assigned packages found no `loadVesselSigningSeed` caller in `packages/lares-cli/src` beyond `commands/meme.ts`; node callers include `open-node-vessel.ts`, `main.ts`, `commands/init.ts`, `commands/nexus-contract.ts`, and `commands/fleet-join.ts`. `device-admit.ts` mentions the loader in a comment but does not itself call it. In meshes, `dyad.ts` accepts the seed as an argument; it does not load disk state.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r5-yang-wave1-humanless-versus-operator-acts`>>

!!!! Humanless versus operator acts


* ''MEASURED — 2026-10-19:'' Boot loads the seed before floor and node composition (`open-node-vessel.ts:945-946`), then derives floor cards/gates and can establish relay and internal node signing capabilities. The `vesselFloorKeyDoor` explicitly answers while locked (`node-vessel-identity.ts:454-470`). A herm can therefore publish/serve the place floor without a face or a person present.
* ''LEAD — 2026-10-19:'' To preserve an unattended herm boot, container restart, and waking floor service, the place signer or a prederived equivalent must be available at boot. A boot custody door would suspend those signer-dependent floor actions until a person unlocks. Public reads not requiring a signature may continue, but exact degraded service needs product-level testing/specification.
* ''MEASURED — 2026-10-19:'' Fleet join, `carry-for`, founding, and promotion CLI paths occur under explicit commands; those can wait for a person. Daemon boot, floor gate reconstruction, boot-time floor card and internal signer setup cannot wait without changing the availability contract.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r5-yang-wave1-q2-consequence-of-seed-theft`>>

!!! Q2 — consequence of seed theft


* ''MEASURED — 2026-10-19:'' A seed thief can sign as the place key anywhere a relying party accepts that key: forge future place carrier seals/floor pins, device/place assertions, floor-card publications, and vessel-authenticated internal asks; derive relay and registered floor-gate children; recreate seed-derived founder veil identities given their derivation tags. The exact reachable operations depend on verifier policy and available public context.
* ''MEASURED — 2026-10-19:'' The place seed is not the custody VK, no VK/FCK derivation is shown, and carrier mapping puts private custody rows behind the custody key (`vault-carriers.ts:239-251`). Thus the place signer alone does not directly open the custody rows or automatically decrypt every face's archives/data. Avoid the stronger claim “cannot expose any face-related material”: it is also supplied to a group-secret opener and dyad-veil derivation path, so targeted sibling/founder-derived material needs a separate cryptographic impact audit.
* ''MEASURED — 2026-10-19:'' The place KEL offers no recovery: `vessel-kel.ts:2-13` and `node-vessel-identity.ts:185-198` explicitly define non-transferable identity, no next-key commitment, and fresh founding after loss/theft. There is no place KEL rotation path in the assigned source; `rg` found only inception/decode/verify. `lares nexus seal rotate` rotates charter seats/epoch, not the place key. Vault `--rotate` rotates custody VK, not this seed.
* ''LEAD — 2026-10-19:'' With current non-rotatable KEL, recovery means abandon/re-found the place identity and re-pin/re-carry it through the relevant Nexus, fleet, and floor-gate relationships. The board can detect signatures that fail, but cannot distinguish attacker signatures made with the stolen valid seed from the real place. Kapae can shadow relationships/edges under their own authorities; it does not recover the compromised place identity or revoke every consumer automatically. Re-found carries continuity and coordination costs.
* ''LEAD — 2026-10-19:'' A named protected signer reduces offline disk theft risk only if the file/keystore boundary actually withholds or cryptographically seals the seed. Owner-only mode `0600` does not protect against root, same-user compromise, backup leakage, or an attacker who can read the boot volume. A TPM/OS-bound design also needs an explicit threat model for a running compromised host.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r5-yang-wave1-place-kel-and-the-proposed-third-shape`>>

!!! Place KEL and the proposed third shape


* ''MEASURED — 2026-10-19:'' Unlike the persona KEL or Nexus charter epoch chain, the place KEL has one self-signed inception and no `n`/next-key digest, threshold, sequence, or successor event. The module comments expressly reject a same-disk next key as offering no theft protection.
* ''LEAD — 2026-10-19:'' A genuinely useful split would put a long-term place root offline and have it certify replaceable boot keys, with explicit expiry/revocation/recovery authority; merely certifying one static boot signer does not help if that signer cannot be revoked. But this changes all verifiers/pins and recovery semantics. A pre-rotation place KEL could make compromise recoverable only if the next private key stays outside the stolen boundary and all consumers enforce the chain.
* ''LEAD — 2026-10-19:'' For the existing code contract, prefer a narrowly named place-signer S protected at rest and auto-available for public floor, while explicitly excluding VK/FCK and documenting non-rotatable identity/re-found recovery. This is a decision recommendation, not a claim that code already meets the protection standard.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r5-yang-wave1-q3-comparison-research-deliberately-deferred`>>

!!! Q3 — comparison research deliberately deferred


* ''MEASURED — 2026-10-19:'' No web research was performed in wave one, as assigned. I therefore make no source-backed claims here about systemd encrypted credentials/TPM2, TPM sealing, Vault auto-unseal, Kubernetes secrets, Tor, WireGuard, SSH host keys, Let's Encrypt, KERI witnesses, or Signal server keys. Those analogies need primary-source review in wave two; do not treat prior-round analogies as this audit's verified comparison.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r5-yang-wave1-ranked-ruling-per-vessel-effect-and-red-proof`>>

!!! Ranked ruling, per-vessel effect, and red proof


# ''LEAD — 2026-10-19 — Rank 1: named place signer, protected at rest, auto-loaded for floor only.'' Keep the floor free of custody VK/FCK; name the signer as a place-identity capability, constrain its APIs to the existing place/floor domains, and protect the seed with an owner-only file at minimum or an OS/TPM keystore where it can actually boot unattended. Document the remaining host-compromise risk and mandatory re-found recovery under today's KEL. This best preserves current herm availability, though code currently uses the signer beyond a narrow signing API (including derivation/group-secret opener), so implementation must narrow/justify each of those uses.
# ''LEAD — 2026-10-19 — Rank 2: split offline root plus replaceable boot signer.'' This offers the strongest recoverability if KEL/verifiers/pins gain a root-certified rotation chain and next authority stays offline. It has a larger migration and compatibility burden; it outranks a simple unlock door for deployments that can operate a carefully provisioned online key.
# ''LEAD — 2026-10-19 — Rank 3: boot custody door around the existing signer.'' It improves protection while powered off but breaks unattended herm/container/waking-floor service until a person arrives. Use only where operator presence at boot is an explicit availability choice. The existing place KEL still cannot rotate after a theft.

| Vessel | Effect (LEAD, 2026-10-19) |
| Herm | Needs unattended place identity and floor gate; rank 1 or a redesigned rank 2 supports it. Rank 3 leaves floor signer-dependent service waiting. |
| Node/hearth | Separate face custody remains behind its own face/VK doors. Place signer protections do not grant or replace face FCKs. Explicit operator acts can wait for unlock. |
| Container | Rank 1/2 allows restart with place identity; no container should restore face VK/FCK from orchestration. Rank 3 makes signer-dependent service wait for a human. |
| Waking floor | Can rederive public gates and carry ciphertext only if the place signer is available under rank 1/2; no derivation implies face-data decryption authority. |

''Red test — LEAD, 2026-10-19:'' From a clean persistent state, boot a faceless herm and restart its container with no human and no VK/SK/FCK. Public floor card, registered floor-gate children, and configured relay identity must reconstruct consistently; outputs/logs expose no seed. Assert every face/custody open and every operation requiring face authority refuses. Then copy only the at-rest image into an attacker-readable environment: the chosen protection must either withhold the signer (protected-store rank 1/2) or explicitly demonstrate/document the clear-file exposure; seed-only access must not unlock custody rows or face archives. For rank 2, additionally rotate the place boot key using the offline root and prove old-key signatures fail after consumers advance, while an offline stolen current seed cannot forge the successor chain.

''Method boundary — MEASURED, 2026-10-19:'' Read-only source inspection and `rg` only. No tests were run, no builds, no git writes, no web search. Principal source paths: `packages/lararium-node/src/node-vessel-identity.ts`, `open-node-vessel.ts`, `carriage-relay.ts`, `commands/nexus-contract.ts`, `commands/fleet-join.ts`, `main.ts`; `packages/lararium-node/src/vault-carriers.ts`; `packages/lararium-mesh/src/vessel-kel.ts`, `dyad.ts`, `persona-group-secret.ts`; `packages/lares-cli/src/commands/meme.ts`.

<<~/ahu>>

