<<~ ahu slot=`#/r5-yin-wave2-trade-b-yin-internet-lens-on-yang-s-code-paths`>>

!!! Trade (b): YIN internet lens on YANG's code paths


''MEASURED — 2026-10-19 — twin-alone:'' The actual implementation is broader than “floor place acts only”: the place seed participates in internal daemon asks/authentication, group-secret opening, and founder veil derivation, as well as public/place signing and relay/floor-gate operations. Therefore the named-floor-signer ruling is a policy boundary that code must enforce or explicitly justify, not a label we can assign to the current seed and declare solved. Do not allow it to open custody rows; independently audit the group-secret and dyad paths for any indirect secret reach.

''LEAD — 2026-10-19 — yours-alone:'' Systems analogies support treating a service identity as a separate named principal, not a face secret. Systemd/TPM and cloud KMS can protect the signer while enabling approved unattended boot, but they still delegate release to the machine/service identity. They do not satisfy human-presence semantics. Kubernetes Secret delivery has configuration-dependent at-rest protection and exposes data to authorized pods at runtime. Tor and SSH demonstrate ordinary persistent service-identity operation, with theft consequences scoped to that identity. WireGuard keys establish peer/network identity and routing scope. ACME account keys authorize account operations under domain-validation and CAA limits. Signal's transparency work can help check consistency of public key mappings, but it neither keeps a server key offline nor grants key recovery. None of these mechanisms implies authority over Lares face rows.

''LEAD — 2026-10-19 — confirmed-by-both:'' Separate the place signer from VK, SK and every face FCK. Require caller-side type/domain allowlists for signer capabilities and independent face proof for D-row operations. A floor signer exception must be named and protected at rest, with the signer/API restricted to place identity and required floor operations. The current `groupSecretOpenerFromSeed` / founder veil use should either be removed from the general floor signer or specified as a distinct, audited capability with explicit data limits.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r5-yin-wave2-q3-primary-source-comparison-scoped-to-this-signer`>>

!!! Q3: primary-source comparison, scoped to this signer


| System | MEASURED promise at rest and boot (2026-10-19) | Lares mapping (LEAD) |
| ''systemd credentials / TPM2'' | `LoadCredentialEncrypted=` decrypts a credential for delivery to the service. systemd credentials can use local TPM2 key, host key, or combination; `SetCredential=` literal data is in a world-readable unit and unsuitable for private material. TPM PCR policy can bind release to boot state. [[systemd Credentials|https://systemd.io/CREDENTIALS/]] and [[systemd-creds manual|https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/257/systemd-creds.html]]. | Good at-rest wrapper for candidate (1), subject to installed systemd/firmware policy. A no-human boot still releases it automatically under machine policy; a compromised authorized running host may use it. |
| ''TPM-sealed key'' | TPM sealing gates unseal on configured policy/state; a PCR policy can bind measured boot. systemd docs demonstrate TPM-backed credential encryption. | Machine-state release reduces copied-disk/wrong-host exposure, not humanless runtime compromise. PIN/presence policy would impede unattended herm startup. |
| ''HashiCorp Vault auto-unseal'' | Vault auto-unseal delegates release to cloud KMS/HSM; Vault connects on startup and supplies encrypted root key for decryption. The delegate substitutes machine/service authorization for human Shamir shares. [[Vault sealing best practices|https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/docs/configuration/seal/seal-best-practices]], [[HSM support|https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/docs/partners/hsm]]. | Analogy to machine-authorized signer release, not a human boot door. Trust shifts to IAM/KMS/HSM and host identity. |
| ''Kubernetes Secrets'' | Secret values are base64 and stored unencrypted in etcd by default; at-rest encryption must be configured. Local encryption keys on control-plane hosts do not protect against host compromise; KMS envelope encryption keeps KEK outside cluster. Mounted/env Secret is available to authorized workload. [[Good practices|https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/secrets-good-practices/]], [[encryption at rest|https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/encrypt-data/]]. | Orchestrator injection alone is not signer protection or human authorization. A pod/host/admin with access can read runtime secret. |
| ''Tor onion-service key'' | v3 identity is a long-term master Ed25519 key; blinded and descriptor keys are distinct. The spec directory format stores private master key in service directory; the proposal describes offline master key as a design possibility and online descriptor signer. [[Tor proposal 224|https://spec.torproject.org/proposals/224-rend-spec-ng.html]]. | Strong service-identity analogy: persistence supports unattended service; compromise impersonates place identity. Key separation can inform root/online signer split. |
| ''WireGuard'' | Peer identity is its public key, private key configures interface, and allowed IPs bind peer keys to routing scope. [[WireGuard paper|https://www.wireguard.com/papers/wireguard.pdf]], [[quick start|https://www.wireguard.com/quickstart/]]. | A stolen key impersonates peer within configured routes; it does not imply access to unrelated face custody. |
| ''OpenSSH host keys'' | `HostKey` points to private host-key file; sshd refuses group/world-readable key files. [[OpenBSD sshd_config|https://man.openbsd.org/sshd_config.5]]. | Owner-only permissions protect against ordinary local users, not root/disk theft; unattended host identity is common but theft enables host impersonation. |
| ''Let's Encrypt / ACME'' | Account ID can be rediscovered by registering with same key; CAA can constrain CA and optionally account URI. CAA/domain validation remains a separate authorization gate. [[Account IDs|https://letsencrypt.org/docs/account-id/]], [[CAA|https://letsencrypt.org/docs/caa/]]. | Key theft grants account-level ability, but account key is not a Lares KEL recovery root. ACME illustrates bounded authority plus independent issuance controls. |
| ''KERI / KERIpy'' | KERI events commit to next-key digests; rotation uses precommitted next keys. The KERI specification says this can regain control after current-key compromise, if next keys remain safe. [[KERI spec|https://trustoverip.github.io/kswg-keri-specification/]], [[KID0005|https://identity.foundation/keri/kids/kid0005Comment.html]], [[KERIpy keeper source|https://github.com/WebOfTrust/keripy/blob/main/src/keri/app/keeping.py]]. | Useful recovery design, not existing Lares behavior. Place KEL currently lacks next commitment or rotation; adding it requires verifier, pin and carry-chain migration. |
| ''Signal'' | Signal's key-transparency service uses log/index structures and independent auditors to detect inconsistent views of user key mappings; PQXDH puts user identity/prekeys on server while private keys stay with user. [[Automatic Key Verification|https://signal.org/blog/automatic-key-verification/]], [[PQXDH spec|https://signal.org/docs/specifications/pqxdh/]]. | Analogy for witnessing public place-key history and keeping face keys separate. Transparency detects equivocation; it does not prevent place-seed theft or itself authorize recovery. |

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r5-yin-wave2-q2-seed-theft-detection-recovery`>>

!!! Q2: seed theft, detection, recovery


''MEASURED — 2026-10-19 — twin-alone:'' A reader of current seed can sign wherever public-key verifiers accept it: place carrier contract/floor pin, vessel/device assertions and internal asks; can derive registered floor-gate children and relay gate seed; and may reproduce seeded founder veil identities when derivation tags are known. The current key file is clear hex, mode 0600 (`node-vessel-identity.ts:140-170`).

''MEASURED — 2026-10-19 — confirmed-by-both:'' Seed is not VK/SK/FCK. The vault carrier table places VK/slot tree and serving-key/serving tree as separate custody mechanisms (`vault-carriers.ts:239-251`); a place seed alone has no demonstrated direct path to open those rows or decrypt all face data. ''Qualification:'' specific face-adjacent group-secret or founder-derived material may be exposed through the explicit seed-fed helpers noted above; do not overclaim zero face-related impact.

''MEASURED — 2026-10-19 — twin-alone:'' Existing place KEL provides no next-key commitment, rotation, or recovery. Therefore it offers no cryptographic way to distinguish legitimate from attacker signatures made with the same stolen seed. Independent transparency witnesses and authenticated pins could reveal inconsistent public histories, but are not in the measured place KEL.

''LEAD — 2026-10-19 — yours-alone:'' Kapae may shadow or sever relationships under its own authority, but cannot undo the compromised signer or automatically revoke every consumer. Under current code the robust recovery path is re-found a new place identity, then re-pin/re-carry/update dependent gates and fleet relations. With an implemented pre-rotation chain, rotate using offline next authority, have consumers verify and advance, then reject old-key signatures; compromise of next/root means re-found. Forgery window persists until consumers accept rotation.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r5-yin-wave2-q4-and-ranked-ruling`>>

!!! Q4 and ranked ruling


''Rank 1 — LEAD — 2026-10-19 — confirmed-by-both: third shape, split root and boot signer with pre-rotated recovery.'' Keep an owner/threshold-controlled place root and committed next key offline; use a replaceable, narrowly scoped online place signer for unattended floor operations. Optionally protect its at-rest credential with TPM/systemd or OS keystore where available. Publish and verify a KEL-like rotation chain across all consumers. This improves theft recovery while preserving service. It is not a quick wrapper change: existing KEL and relying pins do not support this today, and current signer consumers must be narrowed/audited first.

''Rank 2 — LEAD — 2026-10-19 — confirmed-by-both: candidate (1), named floor signer protected at rest.'' Explicitly amend floor law to “no custody VK/FCK; named place signer may be S.” Use owner-only storage at minimum and OS/TPM protection where available; make release policy and unsupported-platform fallback explicit. This aligns with present unattended behavior, but a stolen key currently requires re-found and source shows broader uses than the proposed “place acts only.”

''Rank 3 — LEAD — 2026-10-19 — yours-alone: candidate (2), boot custody door around place signer.'' It makes the signer unavailable until a human arrives, conflicting with faceless Herm and unattended container/waking-floor identity service. Reserve such a door for a distinct sensitive serving S if one is introduced; current table says no serving row stands. If chosen for the place signer, explicitly accept unavailable signed identity until unlock.

| Vessel | Effect of Rank 1 (LEAD, 2026-10-19) |
| Herm | Starts faceless at public floor with online signer; no VK/FCK or face rows. Offline root/next required for place-key rotation. |
| Full node | Same floor identity; face data remains behind face/VK doors. Any genuinely sensitive serving payload gets its own separately named key and unlock policy. |
| Hearth | Inherits node place identity; every face's D rows still require that face's own admission. |
| Container | No-human restart restores only place-floor signer through configured machine protection; it must not restore VK/FCK from images, environment, or orchestration. |
| Waking floor | May carry public identity/proofs/ciphertext unattended; private D decryption or transformation still requires face authority or a separately ruled S. |

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r5-yin-wave2-red-test-lead-2026-10-19`>>

!!!! Red test (LEAD, 2026-10-19)


Run an adversarial no-human boot and copied-disk test. On the authorized host, a faceless Herm and restarted container must publish the expected place card/relay/gate identity; no seed or child secret appears in disk-visible public artifacts, logs, environment, or API. On a copied disk / wrong host, signer release fails if TPM/host binding was selected (or the documented owner-only file exposure is demonstrated if not). With no VK/FCK, attempts to read, replace, restore, rotate, or decrypt any face row fail byte-preservingly. Verify allowlisted signer domains only, and specifically assert that group-secret and dyad derivations cannot become broad custody opens. Then simulate current online-key theft: offline pre-rotated authority emits a valid successor; all consumers advance and reject old-key place acts. If offline next/root is also compromised, require re-found rather than claiming recovery. Candidate (2) variant must assert that no human means no signed place floor identity.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r5-yin-wave2-primary-source-references-and-limits`>>

!!! Primary-source references and limits


All external pages above were read 2026-10-19. Systemd/TPM behavior depends on installed versions, firmware and PCR policy; Kubernetes depends on configured encryption provider and RBAC; KERI is a proposed analogue, not a Lares implementation. Signal evidence concerns user-key transparency, not server host-key storage. This handback does not establish house-specific kapae/re-found procedure, exhaustive transitive effects of group-secret opening, or waking-floor product availability requirements.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r5-yang-wave2-yang-place-signer-handback-wave-two`>>

!! YANG — place signer handback, wave two


Research date: ''2026-10-19''. All claims below dated 2026-10-19. MEASURED = read in repository code or primary source; LEAD = inference/design. Provenance: ''confirmed-by-both'', ''yours-alone'' (YANG/MINE), ''twin-alone'' (YIN/TWIN). Repo stayed read-only; no suite, build, or git write ran. Read `../PRIOR-YIN.md`, `../PRIOR-YANG.md`, then `TWIN.md`; code details in `MINE.md`.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r5-yang-wave2-1-twin-s-principal-claims-checked-against-code`>>

!!! 1. Twin's principal claims checked against code


| Twin claim | Finding and evidence | Provenance |
| Node place seed is private, auto-loaded, independent from VK/SK; Herm receives it. | ''CONFIRM.'' `node-vessel-identity.ts` has distinct `loadVesselSigningSeed`; `open-node-vessel.ts:945,568-582,3283`; carrier table `vault-carriers.ts:239-251`. Qualify: this key also derives/signs capabilities beyond simple place identity (MINE Q1). | confirmed-by-both |
| Herm has zero face slots and serves before any face; SK has no current serving row. | ''CONFIRM.'' Prior code citations `vessel-standing.ts:60-79,113-119`, `vault-carriers.ts:12-14,239-251`, `open-node-vessel.ts:3242-3291`; no code indicates an SK consumer. | confirmed-by-both |
| Private place seed may be called F/public floor data. | ''REFUTE.'' It is private signing material. It can be named separately as S; public verifying key/card may be F. | confirmed-by-both |
| Place KEL can recover by pre-rotation. | ''REFUTE for current code.'' `lararium-mesh/src/vessel-kel.ts:1-17,38-45` explicitly says non-transferable, one-key inception, no next digest/sequence/version; stealing/loss requires re-founding. Charter seat KEL rotation is a different mechanism. | yours-alone |
| Clear current key + offline pre-rotated successor is viable third shape. | ''CONFIRM as LEAD only.'' It is a redesign, not extant behavior; current parser/verifier rejects extra fields / next key. Needs schema, rotation, acceptance/pinning, and independently stored successor. | twin-alone |
| Systemd/TPM can bind a credential to host/boot; auto-release remains unattended authorization. | ''CONFIRM'' from twin's cited systemd primary docs. Fits candidate (1) at-rest strengthening, not human presence. A hostile approved runtime can still ask for/use it. | twin-alone |
| Vault auto-unseal/Kubernetes secret delivery make human boot optional, but shift trust to machine identity/configuration; Kubernetes default etcd storage lacks encryption. | ''CONFIRM'' from sources linked in `TWIN.md`; not Lares face authorization. | twin-alone |
| Tor, WireGuard, SSH host, ACME demonstrate persistent unattended service identity; theft permits impersonation within that key's domain. | ''CONFIRM'' source claims; the analogy supports narrowly typed place identity, not face-row access. | twin-alone |
| Signal transparency protects service private key at rest. | ''REFUTE/qualify.'' Twin correctly notes its transparency service detects inconsistent mappings, distinct from E2E user keys; it does not itself protect the server's own host key or make boot attended. | twin-alone |
| Attacker with seed cannot open face rows. | ''CONFIRM with qualification.'' Distinct custody root/FCK doors reject floor key substitution (`gate-bundle.ts`, node identity). However seed-derived dyad veil and device/Keyhive uses widen impersonation surface. Audit/contain those consumers before claiming “place acts only.” | confirmed-by-both on custody boundary; yours-alone on derived-use qualification |

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r5-yang-wave2-2-yang-code-lens-on-yin-s-ground`>>

!!! 2. YANG code lens on YIN's ground


''MEASURED 2026-10-19:'' The system analogies describe at-rest and boot-release controls; none grants Lares row-key authority. The current mesh floor KEL does not possess KERI pre-rotation. Its format has exactly one self-signed `icp` key and rejects next-key commitment (`vessel-kel.ts:1-17,38-45`). Thus KERI's recovery guarantee cannot be mapped onto today's place key. Nexus charter/seat rotation and Kapae do not repair the place identity: they govern charter seats/participants, not floor KEL. Signal-like public transparency could help detect conflicting announcements, but no place witness/transparency path surfaced in the audited code.

''MEASURED 2026-10-19:'' Candidate (1)'s narrow “place acts only” is not literally true today: seed reaches floor HKDF children, relay gate derivation, `carry-for` seal and floor pin, promotion assertion, device-enrolment signing/opening, and `deriveDyadVeil`; it is supplied into node/Keyhive setup. The red design boundary must inventory and type these uses. The floor gate itself is carefully separate from custody root: both tier doors refuse the other's domain (`gate-bundle.ts:250-310`; `node-vessel-identity.ts`), so the existing typed boundary supports “cannot open custody row,” not “no other credentials derived.”

''LEAD 2026-10-19:'' Map systemd encrypted credentials/TPM to wrapping the place signer at rest and unattended boot release. This may reduce copied-disk theft while preserving Herm uptime; it does not address compromise of a running host. Vault auto-unseal and Kubernetes secret mounts resemble delegated machine release and have similar runtime trust; plain Kubernetes Secret is not enough. Tor/SSH/WireGuard/ACME show how operators accept an always-on service identity and respond to theft by replacing/rebinding public identity. KERI pre-rotation is the closest recovery design only after adding the absent place-KEL machinery. Signal transparency is useful detection inspiration, not key protection.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r5-yang-wave2-3-ranked-ruling-and-per-vessel-effect`>>

!!! 3. Ranked ruling and per-vessel effect


''Rank 1 — third shape: split long-lived place root from scoped boot signer, with independently protected pre-rotated successor. LEAD, 2026-10-19.'' Keep Herm's operational signer online, narrowly authorized for explicitly named floor/place domains. Let an offline owner-held root or threshold process authorize successor rotation; keep next seed off host. Optionally wrap online signer with TPM/systemd credential on supported systems. First remove or separately justify non-place uses (notably dyad veil/face enrollment). This preserves unattended public boot and adds a recovery path; it does not prevent forgery while a current signer remains accepted. Requires new KEL contract, verifier, rotation command, witness/pin update, and recovery runbook.

''Rank 2 — candidate (1): named protected floor signer. LEAD, 2026-10-19.'' Explicitly classify place signer as private floor S, separate from VK/FCK/SK; owner-only file or OS keystore, TPM wrapper when available. Permit only the named place and public floor duties. This is closest to current behavior and preserves herm/container/waking floor availability, but a copied seed can impersonate indefinitely because present KEL cannot rotate. Owner-only mode helps against other local accounts, not disk theft/root.

''Rank 3 — candidate (2): human boot custody door. LEAD, 2026-10-19.'' Stronger protection while powered off, but blocks place identity and floor child derivation until a person unlocks. Keep public card/charter/ciphertext readable if they do not require signer; Herm cannot deliver authenticated floor service before unlock. Use only if operator ranks unattended service below boot-person presence.

| Vessel | Effect under rank 1 / 2 | Effect under rank 3 |
| Herm / crossroads | Auto-start public place identity and floor gates; successor root stays offline. | Waits for person before signer-backed identity/floor endpoints; no-face Herm remains unable to unlock itself. |
| Full node / hearth | Public floor boots unattended; each face row still requires its own face custody. | Node boot door must not be conflated with face unlock; public signer-backed service pauses, face rows remain face-scoped. |
| Container restart | TPM/credential policy releases scoped signer unattended; no face or serving key restored. | Container starts public static floor only; signer services wait for attended unlock. |
| Waking floor | Derive/serve public floor routes, carry ciphertext; never decrypt D rows. | Wakes without signer-backed floor functionality until person arrives. |
| CLI/MCP | Operator acts can request signer-scoped act; face proof remains required for face rows. | CLI may unlock explicitly; MCP must not inherit a prior face or unattended service credential. |

''Red test (LEAD, 2026-10-19):'' fresh zero-face Herm boot and unattended container restart must serve public card/charter and derive only allowlisted floor-gate pins; no VK/FCK/SK or face row opens. Read copied signer file and show attacker can forge current place assertion, then rotate through separately held next authority and prove old key fails while new KEL verifies; attempted floor-door sovereign derivation and cross-face decrypt must refuse. Repeat on rank 2 without offline rotation to demonstrate expected permanent-key recovery gap; rank 3 must demonstrably refuse signer-backed endpoints before human unlock while leaving public unsigned bytes readable.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/r5-yang-wave2-4-decision-critical-caveat`>>

!!! 4. Decision-critical caveat


Current seed's direct and derived uses exceed the candidate's “place acts only” statement. A ruling for named floor signer should either narrow the code capability passed from `vesselSeed` or enumerate every approved domain, including floor gate derivation, relay, carriage, promotion, enrollment, and dyad veil. Custody independence appears strong in typed gate doors, but that alone does not neutralize credential/identity misuse. No implementation changes or tests were requested or performed.

<<~/ahu>>

